answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315864} {:answer/id #uuid "f2f486c4-6a97-5a85-bb1d-c46b9ac5c8b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316471}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85cff5d8-8660-5f09-837d-b9a7c81dfdb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315864} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec2fdc4d-025d-5256-ba3f-588326c84d79" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317105}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a67d7600-1f79-59aa-9051-5cda440b9948" :question/math-academy-id "q-343901" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}y=f(x),$ where the $4$-periodic forcing function is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼-2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below regarding the resonant component of a particular solution of the equation.\n\nThe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the {{field-1}} harmonic of the forcing function. \n\nConsequently, the particular solution contains a resonant term of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}={{field-2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $4$-periodic function $(L=2)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=1$, we have ${\\omega }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Since ${\\omega }_{1}=\\omega ,$ the $n=1$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=1$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{1}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}))+x(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+\\frac{\\pi }{2}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{1}}^{″} & =-\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{1}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{1}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}y=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}):$\n\n$$\n[-\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}{y}_{{p}_{1}}]+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}{y}_{{p}_{1}}=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\pi A &=0 \\\\ \\pi B &=6\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}A &=0 \\\\ B &=\\frac{6}{\\pi }\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}=\\frac{6x}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317452 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5229753e-9f7f-5a27-b3ee-c4a1ce8dde6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315586} {:answer/id #uuid "862b0cf3-99de-5116-b9ab-82a11436ad9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315868} {:answer/id #uuid "d2dff446-91ec-5570-8171-c24135981f75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316299} {:answer/id #uuid "d9600449-c9b9-5aec-ba8b-15aa0966e5da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316328}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "862b0cf3-99de-5116-b9ab-82a11436ad9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315868} :answer-field/id #uuid "d87d8604-1b8f-56e7-a069-08f0654c46b0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317060} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d996731-b251-5b34-875b-e57bb6c2b7c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{15x}{8\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315290} {:answer/id #uuid "3a5d9269-3d3c-576a-b059-ab2e729a7735" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{15x}{16\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315453} {:answer/id #uuid "f4cc4c52-e7e1-5f1b-ab95-62c5cbd519d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15x}{16\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316484} {:answer/id #uuid "fce744b4-f140-59b2-84b8-c7432217b040" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{15x}{32\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316532}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a5d9269-3d3c-576a-b059-ab2e729a7735" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{15x}{16\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315453} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb46e197-216d-53cb-9e25-46577a9846a0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317103}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "de35c174-f52d-54b1-9320-5816b5069f2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-343905" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}y=f(x),$ where the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼-3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below regarding the resonant component of a particular solution of the equation.\n\nThe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the {{field-1}} harmonic of the forcing function. \n\nConsequently, the particular solution contains a resonant term of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}={{field-2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $6$-periodic function $(L=3)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=2$, we have ${\\omega }_{2}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$ Since ${\\omega }_{2}=\\omega ,$ the $n=2$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=2$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x)=\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{y}_{{p}_{2}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}))+x(-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})) \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{y}_{{p}_{2}}^{″} & =-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{4\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})-\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{2}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{2}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}y=\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}):$\n\n$$\n[-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{4\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})-\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}{y}_{{p}_{2}}]+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}{y}_{{p}_{2}}=\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{4\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})=\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A &=\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ \\frac{4\\pi }{3}B &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}A &=-\\frac{15}{16\\pi } \\\\ B &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}=-\\frac{15x}{16\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317453 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fe6e1ef-2a02-59be-967b-360af6440b14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315213} {:answer/id #uuid "2493fd79-af96-5964-b4d5-79c55216b0d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315324} {:answer/id #uuid "acf5a8df-8734-5bf9-9bcc-688e3e570e08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316092} {:answer/id #uuid "ae1a6513-a24b-5275-9db3-0cd9e31e4d3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316104}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0fe6e1ef-2a02-59be-967b-360af6440b14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315213} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b3c4e4e-5751-5f8c-b537-c9c21b1bec65" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316808} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "113bb42a-cce4-59ec-8300-8cfaa0e4ff29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315221} {:answer/id #uuid "514af182-9b72-5c63-aec6-2e477be64e8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315582} {:answer/id #uuid "764ddf91-8316-5d5f-ae60-fa33202df071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315785} {:answer/id #uuid "82fa34d1-254e-5284-8f46-340093da20bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{4\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315841}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "514af182-9b72-5c63-aec6-2e477be64e8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315582} :answer-field/id #uuid "f855de14-5251-533c-9172-a67d92d9e753" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317132}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f7afbc4a-cfc0-549a-b6ce-77b93f2927bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-343907" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x),$ where the $4$-periodic forcing function is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below regarding the resonant component of a particular solution of the equation.\n\nThe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the {{field-1}} harmonic of the forcing function. \n\nConsequently, the particular solution contains a resonant term of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}={{field-2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}}=\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $4$-periodic function $(L=2)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=2$, we have ${\\omega }_{2}=\\pi .$ Since ${\\omega }_{2}=\\omega ,$ the $n=2$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=2$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{2}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x))+x(-\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{2}}^{″} & =-2\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+2\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)-{\\pi }^{2}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{2}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{2}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+{\\pi }^{2}y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x):$\n\n$$\n[-2\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+2\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)-{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{2}}]+{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{2}}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-2\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+2\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2\\pi A &=1 \\\\ 2\\pi B &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}A &=-\\frac{1}{2\\pi } \\\\ B &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}=-\\frac{x}{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317461 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30ec1724-e787-53ac-b7be-da19606b22cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315398}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30ec1724-e787-53ac-b7be-da19606b22cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315398} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b8c06e6-1c97-596f-a48b-c8e3a08ea23e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316809}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f5c39cbb-58a7-550a-8949-adbe8e453b51" :question/math-academy-id "q-344033" :question/problem "Consider the section of the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1$ that lies in the first quadrant.  Given that $x=x(t),$ $y=y(t),$  and that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=3$ on the ellipse at the point where $y=3,$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16})+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (\\frac{2x}{16})+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (\\frac{2y}{25}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, substituting the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=3$ and $y=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (\\frac{2x}{16})+3\\cdot (\\frac{2(3)}{25}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{x}{8}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{18}{25} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also want to substitute for $x,$ but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $y=3$ into the equation of the ellipse.  \n\nAt the point where $y=3,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{3}^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{9}{25} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{16}{25} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{256}{25} \\\\ x & =\\frac{16}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used $x>0$ since we're only interested in the first quadrant. \n\nFinally, substituting $x=\\frac{16}{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{8}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{18}{25} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{8}(\\frac{16}{5})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{18}{25} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{18}{25} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{18}{25}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{9}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317462 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2d6b70a-1c88-5078-851d-20d14bc97f70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316130}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2d6b70a-1c88-5078-851d-20d14bc97f70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316130} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba1a5d6f-aeae-536f-a6b0-a2ebf0eb504b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316985} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c6bac51-594e-5c2f-9f7b-596126ee5877" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315646}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c6bac51-594e-5c2f-9f7b-596126ee5877" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315646} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0afef72-f33d-5803-bb02-33e8a33d9345" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317120}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "16454d27-1535-5826-aeea-dc3e22b21a41" :question/math-academy-id "q-344059" :question/problem "In **ascending order**, the $x$-coordinates of the points of inflection for $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+x$ on the interval $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,\\text{and}\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+1 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0$ on the interval $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x) & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{8},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check if the concavity changes at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ and $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{8}.$ So, we pick points around them, say $x=\\frac{\\pi }{16},$ $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{16}) & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=-16\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )=-16\\cdot (-1)>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )=-16\\cdot (1)<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ and $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{8},$ we conclude that, in ascending order, \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\,\\text{and}\\,x=\\frac{3\\pi }{8}\n$$\n\n are indeed points of inflection."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317463 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40cfd269-a13a-5c49-a56d-cc8ceb8431d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315492}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40cfd269-a13a-5c49-a56d-cc8ceb8431d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315492} :answer-field/id #uuid "77bf0f74-1242-55a2-a21a-8980e84f9457" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316833}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "182e0255-d720-51ea-a804-063597c68a5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-344063" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)={x}^{2}+3x-2+\\frac{1}{x-2}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, notice that $f(x)$ is not defined at $x=2,$ so this can't be a point of inflection.\n\nNow, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =2x+3-\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2+\\frac{2}{(x-2)^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2+\\frac{2}{(x-2)^{3}} & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{3} & =-1 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=1.$ So, we pick two points around it, say, $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{3}{2}$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0) & =2+\\frac{2}{(0-2)^{3}}=\\frac{7}{4}>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(\\frac{3}{2}) & =2+\\frac{2}{{(\\frac{3}{2}-2)}^{3}}=-14<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=1,$ we conclude that $x=1$ is indeed a point of inflection."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317464 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6285fdf-3425-5746-8f76-fcb1a2e4790d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316392}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6285fdf-3425-5746-8f76-fcb1a2e4790d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316392} :answer-field/id #uuid "02baa181-4cae-5b82-a453-d233c9ccf753" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316554}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8a56a03b-dc7c-5786-95b6-2f30ee5aa6c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-344064" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)=27{x}^{2}+5x-4+\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, notice that $f(x)$ is not defined at $x=0,$ so this can't be a point of inflection.\n\nNow, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =54x+5-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =54+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}54+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =-\\frac{1}{27} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=-\\frac{1}{3}.$ So, we pick two points around it, say, $x=-1$ and $x=-\\frac{1}{6}$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-1) & =54+\\frac{2}{(-1)^{3}}=52>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(-\\frac{1}{6}) & =54+\\frac{2}{{(-\\frac{1}{6})}^{3}}=-378<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=-\\frac{1}{3},$ we conclude that $x=-\\frac{1}{3}$ is indeed a point of inflection."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317470 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a149596-45cb-5497-baab-8a480b36b3cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315980}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a149596-45cb-5497-baab-8a480b36b3cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315980} :answer-field/id #uuid "185a3b06-7b04-557c-9acd-c47afbe20d98" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316601}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "43806c2b-bb8d-5959-9b41-a6ace76db4b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-344144" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\pi /6}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x)-\\sqrt{3}}{2}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\pi /6}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x)-\\sqrt{3}}{2} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})-\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}-\\sqrt{3})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{0}{1}-\\sqrt{3})}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317501 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dda6c739-c951-540d-9d06-ed45e64a9ccd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316351}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dda6c739-c951-540d-9d06-ed45e64a9ccd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316351} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa2a80d5-52b8-5a6f-b526-e3afdc2463d0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316943}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "90cdcdfc-8cef-516d-ad76-cccdc4fcb4b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-344443" :question/problem "The global minimum value of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ on the closed interval $0\\le x\\le 2\\pi $ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer. We discard all solutions except $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\frac{3\\pi }{2},$ since they are the only values that belongs to the given interval.\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ and the endpoints $x=0,2\\pi $ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)=0 \\\\ f\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1 \\\\ f\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) & =sin\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=-1 \\\\ f(2\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1,$ so the global maximum is $1.$\n\n- The least value is $f\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=-1,$ so the global minimum is $-1.$\n\n![](images/c5/c5368ebe232e9ad044cacb16300fb2f3f1747fefd326f4be4e0c22d0e3a7069d.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317505 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bab09168-9eed-5784-9dce-96666fa7b241" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316173}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bab09168-9eed-5784-9dce-96666fa7b241" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316173} :answer-field/id #uuid "4404a6c2-204a-5433-be94-2569b39115f6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316701}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fda2ab6f-26df-547c-acfa-52247e6d9f09" :question/math-academy-id "q-344477" :question/problem "The exact global maximum value of the function $f(x)=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ on the closed interval $0\\le x\\le \\pi $ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & =0 \\\\ 2x & =\\pi n \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}n,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer. We discard all solutions except $x=0,$ $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ and $x=\\pi ,$ since they are the only values that belong to the given interval.\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\pi ,$ and the endpoints $x=0,\\,\\pi $ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=0 \\\\ f\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )=1-(-1)=2 \\\\ f(\\pi ) & =1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=2,$ so the global maximum is $2.$\n\n- The least value is $f(0)=0,$ so the global minimum is $0.$\n\n![](images/6b/6b8ac504d93007049795ee92eb8865225751314478d68ace67878e27ed471b24.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317512 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e56fed0a-d8f9-5c20-b0a0-6a076d63dfbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316389}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e56fed0a-d8f9-5c20-b0a0-6a076d63dfbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316389} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e11ff29-510f-539f-968f-2aea79bad506" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316726}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3ee597c9-1d21-553e-8a89-39a826142bb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-344578" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}}tan\\,(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)-\\frac{\\pi }{6})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x):$\n\n![](images/ca/ca5e8035505f20619b6707d6d1f2d0b7b2f125a004a0dbd9d448840b0e4ffd99.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, substituting $x=\\sqrt{3}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}}tan\\,(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =tan\\,(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317518 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85f1b892-f0a8-59e3-9b07-49d53287c44b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315865}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85f1b892-f0a8-59e3-9b07-49d53287c44b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315865} :answer-field/id #uuid "e50b65dd-c817-5385-8da8-d45b1fdf82fc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317092}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "75f800d2-5795-5843-aa0e-4fbc76c0c3f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-344635" :question/problem "What is the smallest possible distance between the points $(4x,\\,-x)$ and $(5,\\,-1),$ where $x$ is a real number? Do not round the answer.\n\nThe smallest possible distance is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's compute the *squared* distance $S(x)$ between the two points. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & ={((4x)-5)}^{2}+{((-x)-(-1))}^{2} \\\\  & =(4x-5)^{2}+(-x+1)^{2} \\\\  & =(16{x}^{2}-40x+25)+({x}^{2}-2x+1) \\\\  & =17{x}^{2}-42x+26.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want to minimize $S,$ we compute the derivative ${S}^{′}(x).$ This gives\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(x)=34x-42.\n$$\n\nThe stationary points of $S(x)$ are the points where the derivative ${S}^{′}(x)=0.$ Solving this equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 34x-42 & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{21}{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}\\,(\\frac{21}{17})=34>0.$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) reaches a minimum when $x=\\frac{21}{17}.$ \n\nSince $S(x)$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=\\frac{21}{17}$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S\\,(\\frac{21}{17})}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S\\,(\\frac{21}{17})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17{(\\frac{21}{17})}^{2}-42(\\frac{21}{17})+26} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{441}{17}-\\frac{882}{17}+26} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{17}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the smallest possible distance between the points $(4x,\\,-x)$ and $(5,\\,-1)$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317519 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad21b14d-725c-5ae1-a6d6-1747733934f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316093}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad21b14d-725c-5ae1-a6d6-1747733934f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316093} :answer-field/id #uuid "98ee24d5-28af-5c35-8a1f-502a26d5751a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316896}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "31b8934f-056b-557b-af2b-bed30fb7f24e" :question/math-academy-id "q-344637" :question/problem "What is the smallest possible distance between the points $(-4x,\\,2x-3)$ and $(1,\\,5),$ where $x$ is a real number? Do not round the answer.\n\nThe smallest possible distance is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's compute the *squared* distance $S(x)$ between the two points. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & ={((-4x)-1)}^{2}+{((2x-3)-5)}^{2} \\\\  & =(-4x-1)^{2}+(2x-8)^{2} \\\\  & =16{x}^{2}+8x+1+4{x}^{2}-32x+64 \\\\  & =20{x}^{2}-24x+65.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want to minimize $S,$ we compute the derivative ${S}^{′}(x).$ This gives\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(x)=40x-24.\n$$\n\nThe stationary points of $S(x)$ are the points where the derivative ${S}^{′}(x)=0.$ Solving this equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 40x-24 & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(\\frac{3}{5})=40>0.$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) reaches a minimum when $x=\\frac{3}{5}.$ \n\nSince $S(x)$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=\\frac{3}{5}$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S(\\frac{3}{5})}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S(\\frac{3}{5})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{20{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}-24(\\frac{3}{5})+65} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{289}{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{17\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the smallest possible distance between the points $(-4x,\\,2x-3)$ and $(1,\\,5)$ is $\\frac{17\\sqrt{5}}{5}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317520 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ea414ab-9284-54af-a20c-df1a6000dc0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316011}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ea414ab-9284-54af-a20c-df1a6000dc0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316011} :answer-field/id #uuid "993ddb6b-bead-5cb7-9df2-2c6411c032f1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316899}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "18e30c68-29e4-5882-9787-b249c623ee70" :question/math-academy-id "q-344638" :question/problem "What is the smallest possible distance between the points $(2a-1,\\,a)$ and $(3,\\,-4),$ where $a$ is a real number? Do not round the answer.\n\nThe smallest possible distance is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's compute the *squared* distance $S(a)$ between the two points. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(a) & ={((2a-1)-3)}^{2}+{(a-(-4))}^{2} \\\\  & =(2a-4)^{2}+(a+4)^{2} \\\\  & =4{a}^{2}-16a+16+{a}^{2}+8a+16 \\\\  & =5{a}^{2}-8a+32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want to minimize $S,$ we compute the derivative ${S}^{′}(a).$ This gives\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(a)=10a-8.\n$$\n\nThe stationary points of $S(a)$ are the points where the derivative ${S}^{′}(a)=0.$ Solving this equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(a) & =0 \\\\ 10a-8 & =0 \\\\ a & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(\\frac{4}{5})=10>0.$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) reaches a minimum when $a=\\frac{4}{5}.$ \n\nSince $S(a)$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $a=\\frac{4}{5}$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S(\\frac{4}{5})}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S(\\frac{4}{5})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2}-8(\\frac{4}{5})+32} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{144}{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the smallest possible distance between the points $(2a-1,\\,a)$ and $(3,\\,-4)$ is $\\frac{12\\sqrt{5}}{5}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317521 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d627fcc8-0c7c-5ec5-823a-9af202f649e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316314}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d627fcc8-0c7c-5ec5-823a-9af202f649e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316314} :answer-field/id #uuid "fadcedd9-8a28-542e-a87b-f42080eaf224" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317141}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ca22e7ac-d65b-5ebd-9780-abfeab066eda" :question/math-academy-id "q-344648" :question/problem "Find the average value ${f}_{\\text{avg}}$ of the function $f(x)={cos}^{6}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ on the interval $[0,\\pi ].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${f}_{\\text{avg}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Applying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi -0}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{6}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate this integral using a substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\pi $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{6}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{-1}{u}^{6}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-{\\frac{1}{\\pi }(\\frac{{u}^{7}}{7})|}_{1}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{7\\pi }[(-1)^{7}-{1}^{7}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{7\\pi }(-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{7\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317522 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d86bc992-e7c7-51a6-973a-68223fd836d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316324}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d86bc992-e7c7-51a6-973a-68223fd836d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316324} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f0e99b7-59eb-5b5a-9381-533f823ee997" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316916}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2725b28f-9ef7-5d99-8e23-3b5d9088cbb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-344661" :question/problem "The radius of a circle is increasing. At a particular moment, the rate of increase of the area of the circle is numerically equal to nine times the rate of increase of the circumference. What is the diameter $D$ of the circle at that moment?\n\n$D=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $r$ be the radius, $A$ the area, and $C$ the circumference of the circle. We wish to find $D$ at the moment when $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=9\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the area $A$ and the circumference $C$ of the circle. Since $C=2\\pi r$ and $A=\\pi {r}^{2},$ the relationship between $A$ and $C$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}=\\pi {(\\frac{C}{2\\pi })}^{2}=\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{C}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=9\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{C}{2\\pi } \\\\ 9 & =\\frac{C}{2\\pi } \\\\ C & =18\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, since $C=2\\pi r,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi r & =18\\pi  \\\\ r & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D=2r,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =2\\cdot 9 \\\\ D & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317523 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80e85d85-d317-56a3-9df1-af7d8fa3af0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315828}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80e85d85-d317-56a3-9df1-af7d8fa3af0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315828} :answer-field/id #uuid "99ec8c43-cdaa-5daa-9615-b126e0f71e7f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316901}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "75615dd3-8f3f-5737-be64-7bd427629c46" :question/math-academy-id "q-344670" :question/problem "The radius of a circle is increasing. At a particular moment, the rate of increase of the area of the circle is numerically equal to three times the rate of increase of the circumference. What is the radius $r$ of the circle at that moment? Do not round the answer.\n\n$r=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $r$ be the radius, $A$ the area, and $C$ the circumference of the circle. We wish to find $r$ at the moment when $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=3\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the area $A$ and the circumference $C$ of the circle. Since $C=2\\pi r$ and $A=\\pi {r}^{2},$ the relationship between $A$ and $C$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}=\\pi {(\\frac{C}{2\\pi })}^{2}=\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{C}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=3\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{C}{2\\pi } \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{C}{2\\pi } \\\\ C & =6\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, since $C=2\\pi r,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi r & =6\\pi  \\\\ r & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317525 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b4ee107-21db-5689-85c9-b80b06f4c3d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arccos}⁡({e}^{x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315181} {:answer/id #uuid "4c0e1dae-01af-595c-97b0-0e37d88bbd7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315552} {:answer/id #uuid "6616c1ae-007b-5b69-8440-76d0243d9b28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡({e}^{x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315686} {:answer/id #uuid "8ba614e3-4f54-5c6a-95bd-ebddc37e1c00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315899} {:answer/id #uuid "90d6ab86-ee65-535c-a234-282f92588ef5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({e}^{x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315926}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90d6ab86-ee65-535c-a234-282f92588ef5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({e}^{x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315926} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2162442-b858-55b1-8875-ff547912668d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317041}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "03a6ce91-adf7-5501-8df5-e5f2d7fbd98e" :question/math-academy-id "q-344685" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{‴}+{y}^{″}-10{y}^{′}+8y=\\frac{6{e}^{3x}}{1+{e}^{2x}},\\,{{y}_{1}={e}^{-4x},\\,{y}_{2}={e}^{x},\\,{y}_{3}={e}^{2x}}\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation and the set of fundamental solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation above. \nUsing the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){e}^{-4x}+{u}_{2}(x){e}^{x}+{u}_{3}(x){e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the function ${u}_{3}(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "According to the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}+{u}_{3}{y}_{3},\\,{u}_{i}=(-1)^{3-i}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{i}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,i=1,2,3,\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is the forcing term, $W(x)$ is the Wronskian of ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{1}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}|,\\,{W}_{2}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}|,\\,{W}_{3}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the forcing term is $g(x)=\\frac{6{e}^{3x}}{1+{e}^{2x}}.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}={e}^{-4x},\\,{y}_{2}={e}^{x},\\,{y}_{3}={e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(x) & =W({y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3}) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-4x} & {e}^{x} & {e}^{2x} \\\\ -4{e}^{-4x} & {e}^{x} & 2{e}^{2x} \\\\ 16{e}^{-4x} & {e}^{x} & 4{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =({e}^{-4x}\\cdot {e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{2x})|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 16 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}[1(4-2)-1(-16-32)+1(-4-16)] \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}(2-(-48)+(-20)) \\\\  & =30{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, to find ${u}_{3},$ we compute the determinant\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{3}(x) & ={W}_{3}({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-4x} & {e}^{x} \\\\ -4{e}^{-4x} & {e}^{x}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{-3x}-(-4{e}^{-3x}) \\\\  & =5{e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{3}(x) & =(-1)^{3-3}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{3}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{(\\frac{6{e}^{3x}}{1+{e}^{2x}})(5{e}^{-3x})}{30{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{1+{({e}^{x})}^{2}}\\cdot {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({e}^{x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317526 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9578200c-80e3-5535-9d6f-91e9bbe3c55c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315947} {:answer/id #uuid "d2f4b4df-8748-5825-bdf7-90884a1e0861" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316301} {:answer/id #uuid "eb5403ba-094e-58e3-b5d2-18c221dcb438" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316424} {:answer/id #uuid "edcdfda0-8659-58c6-8f73-ffd68d4549eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316439} {:answer/id #uuid "ffefbff7-2888-5352-a95c-597c6a5eef24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316547}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2f4b4df-8748-5825-bdf7-90884a1e0861" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316301} :answer-field/id #uuid "d390e9bd-ba09-5cd4-a166-961c9dd00035" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317046}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5d7cae11-f1a1-5bb4-a4fd-a26a4496b37f" :question/math-academy-id "q-344686" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{‴}-{y}^{″}-12{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}},\\,{{y}_{1}=1,\\,{y}_{2}={e}^{-3x},\\,{y}_{3}={e}^{4x}}\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation and the set of fundamental solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation above. \nUsing the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x)\\cdot 1+{u}_{2}(x){e}^{-3x}+{u}_{3}(x){e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the function ${u}_{1}(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "According to the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}+{u}_{3}{y}_{3},\\,{u}_{i}=(-1)^{3-i}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{i}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,i=1,2,3,\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is the forcing term, $W(x)$ is the Wronskian of ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{1}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}|,\\,{W}_{2}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}|,\\,{W}_{3}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the forcing term is $g(x)=\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}=1,\\,{y}_{2}={e}^{-3x},\\,{y}_{3}={e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(x) & =W({y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3}) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & {e}^{-3x} & {e}^{4x} \\\\ 0 & -3{e}^{-3x} & 4{e}^{4x} \\\\ 0 & 9{e}^{-3x} & 16{e}^{4x}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(1\\cdot {e}^{-3x}\\cdot {e}^{4x})|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 16\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{x}[1(-48-36)-1(0-0)+1(0-0)] \\\\  & =-84{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are linearly independent.\n\nNow, to find ${u}_{1},$ we compute the determinant\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{1}(x) & ={W}_{1}({y}_{2},{y}_{3}) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3x} & {e}^{4x} \\\\ -3{e}^{-3x} & 4{e}^{4x}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4{e}^{x}-(-3{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =7{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =(-1)^{3-1}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{1}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{(\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}})(7{e}^{x})}{(-84{e}^{x})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}\\int (\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317528 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed2abe4d-57c0-599d-b2da-3bc3a8e57a74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316437} {:answer/id #uuid "9019925a-fde9-5e42-9741-5b09732117ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\pi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516414}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9019925a-fde9-5e42-9741-5b09732117ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\pi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516414} :answer-field/id #uuid "492b509c-6314-53ae-a2da-64e59ff1f2cb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316716}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ce1dc838-ac3c-58a3-9be3-5a30e3211516" :question/math-academy-id "q-344695" :question/problem "Find the average value ${f}_{\\text{avg}}$ of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ on the interval $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{3}].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${f}_{\\text{avg}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Applying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-0)}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{3}{\\pi }(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3})|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\pi }[(-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ))-(-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\pi }[(-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-1))-(-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 1)] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\pi }(\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317529 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13e3bddb-3f9c-576e-a683-adad59e375a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "150" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13e3bddb-3f9c-576e-a683-adad59e375a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "150" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315237} :answer-field/id #uuid "49f84908-ceb0-5d12-b800-1a1ccc31bffa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316719}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "74174cfe-6c69-5c75-8eeb-a70591a0502f" :question/math-academy-id "q-344699" :question/problem "A biologist found that the population $P$ of some algae in a container can be modeled by the function $P(t)=150+10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{3}),$ where $t$ is the number of hours after $9$ a.m. Find the average number of algae during the period from $9$ a.m. to $3$ p.m. Do not round the answer.\n\n${P}_{\\text{avg}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since the function is valid starting from $9$ A.M., we can conclude our interval is $[0,6].$\n\nApplying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}P(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6-0}{\\int }_{0}^{6}(150+10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{3}))\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{6}(150t-\\frac{30}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi \\,t}{3}))|}_{0}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[(150\\cdot 6-\\frac{30}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi \\cdot 6}{3}))-(150\\cdot 0-\\frac{30}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi \\cdot 0}{3}))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[(900-\\frac{30}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi ))-(-\\frac{30}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[(900-\\frac{30}{\\pi }\\cdot 1)+\\frac{30}{\\pi }\\cdot 1] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(900) \\\\  & =150.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317552 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32429789-340a-5002-b533-a0926ca7655b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315410}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32429789-340a-5002-b533-a0926ca7655b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315410} :answer-field/id #uuid "3825048c-0fc0-51d5-9eb1-3d7ec2a1a9ce" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316678}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ec33b4cc-e601-5542-983e-0d1fb7c5da6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-344839" :question/problem "![](images/41/4108c44d3fac12cc38c0aab73ed1600f034ad88c1c50be91ea7bb29145ce4dd7.png)\n\nFind the least distance $d$ between the curve $y=\\sqrt{|x-1|}+3$ and the point $(3,3).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$d=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(3,3)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\nS=(x-3)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{|x-1|}+3.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =(x-3)^{2}+(y-3)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-3)^{2}+(\\sqrt{|x-1|}+3-3)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-3)^{2}+(\\sqrt{|x-1|})^{2} \\\\  & =(x-3)^{2}+|x-1|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x\\ge 1,$ we have $|x-1|=x-1,$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =(x-3)^{2}+(x-1) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-6x+9+x-1 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-5x+8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-5 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =5 \\\\ x & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(x)=2>0,$ so $x=\\frac{5}{2}$ gives a minimum of $S.$\n\nSince $S$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=\\frac{5}{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S\\,(\\frac{5}{2})}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S\\,(\\frac{5}{2})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}-5(\\frac{5}{2})+8} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317559 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78e9fedb-8ab0-5701-acb6-a9684f9b246d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315795}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78e9fedb-8ab0-5701-acb6-a9684f9b246d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315795} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae2edd7b-0e17-5f67-97f1-9cc36dda5b1d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316956} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31830d60-572b-53c9-beb3-866632f93171" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315402}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31830d60-572b-53c9-beb3-866632f93171" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315402} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3869e88-b658-5625-8b8f-173aec9fd973" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317045}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aad5d828-6e42-525d-9e43-aca27206e282" :question/math-academy-id "q-344867" :question/problem "In **ascending order**, the critical points of the function  $f(x)=\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{2}+8}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{2}+8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)^{′}\\cdot ({x}^{2}+8)-(x+1)\\cdot ({x}^{2}+8)^{′}}{({x}^{2}+8)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1)\\cdot ({x}^{2}+8)-(x+1)\\cdot (2x)}{({x}^{2}+8)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+8-(2{x}^{2}+2x)}{({x}^{2}+8)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{x}^{2}-2x+8}{({x}^{2}+8)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-{x}^{2}-2x+8}{({x}^{2}+8)^{2}} & =0 \\\\ -{x}^{2}-2x+8 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x-8 & =0 \\\\ (x+4)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-4,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-4$ and $x=2.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So, $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the critical points of $f(x)$ are \n\n$$\nx=-4\\,\\text{and}\\,x=2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317562 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27faa0ca-a455-52fd-9dd8-02fe35ba628a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315347}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27faa0ca-a455-52fd-9dd8-02fe35ba628a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315347} :answer-field/id #uuid "20384305-0225-53ad-8371-37c723310e1d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316622}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "685db69b-5eb1-53eb-860a-8d2e205a080e" :question/math-academy-id "q-344894" :question/problem "![](images/bd/bd78704be61e30879d4e659a255f93f5c8b6e560951d06b23cf547c8846b01ee.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=h(x)$ are given above. Find ${p}^{′}(2)$ where $p(x)=f(x)h(x).$\n\n${p}^{′}(2)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The product rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{p}^{′}(x)={f}^{′}(x)\\cdot h(x)+f(x)\\cdot {h}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{p}^{′}(2)={f}^{′}(2)\\cdot h(2)+f(2)\\cdot {h}^{′}(2).\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(2)=\\frac{3}{2}$ and $h(2)=0.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (1,3)$ equals $\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, ${f}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The slope of $h(x)$ on $x\\in (1,3)$ equals $2.$ So, ${h}^{′}(2)=2.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{′}(2) & ={f}^{′}(2)\\cdot h(2)+f(2)\\cdot {h}^{′}(2) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot 0+(\\frac{3}{2})\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317563 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0edef33-d799-5790-86fb-0539a3ff15d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316122}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0edef33-d799-5790-86fb-0539a3ff15d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316122} :answer-field/id #uuid "2911a64d-bae7-5526-856a-ae2da769f80c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316641}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a3365bab-5c03-5432-8787-0f1fa7a1200c" :question/math-academy-id "q-344896" :question/problem "![](images/84/847d1ae3ff7602ae8793ec356dd3034650d8ba85adcb3be579097e46728667a0.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${p}^{′}(3)$ where $p(x)=f(x)g(x).$\n\n${p}^{′}(3)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The product rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{p}^{′}(x)={f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)+f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{p}^{′}(3)={f}^{′}(3)\\cdot g(3)+f(3)\\cdot {g}^{′}(3).\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(3)=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $g(3)=1.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (2,4)$ equals $\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, ${f}^{′}(3)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (2,4)$ equals $2.$ So, ${g}^{′}(3)=2.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{′}(3) & ={f}^{′}(3)\\cdot g(3)+f(3)\\cdot {g}^{′}(3) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot 1+(\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317567 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3dedc46b-3634-5c19-916d-49b508376551" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315477}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3dedc46b-3634-5c19-916d-49b508376551" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315477} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e9759a7-3dfa-515a-97e9-c17500231277" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316657}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "204c07af-7a93-54e7-bec3-21115302d481" :question/math-academy-id "q-344914" :question/problem "![](images/f2/f23d8acd8b59e630987fd9a04b71a5e0813f5c084f22489af342ba04c34eb003.png)\n\nGiven the plots of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ above, find ${h}^{′}(-1)$ where $h(x)=\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.$\n\n${h}^{′}(-1)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The quotient rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)-f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(-1)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(-1)\\cdot g(-1)-f(-1)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-1)}{(g(-1))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(-1)=1$ and $g(-1)=2.$\n\nAdditionally:\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (-3,1)$ equals $1.$ So, ${f}^{′}(-1)=1.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (-3,1)$ equals $\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, ${g}^{′}(-1)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(-1) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(-1)\\cdot g(-1)-f(-1)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-1)}{(g(-1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 2-1\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})}{(2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{3}{2}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318962 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "337b07ce-a0b3-58ed-91b9-95871831116b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317730}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "337b07ce-a0b3-58ed-91b9-95871831116b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317730} :answer-field/id #uuid "37e86e47-e92b-5203-841b-21c8339313e3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318540}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a9eb0f85-ea20-5408-b6ef-a6a801e3cb2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-345063" :question/problem "Find the **exact** value of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for $f(x)=5\\sqrt{x}-2$ on the interval $[4,9].$\n\n$c=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[4,9]$ and differentiable on $(4,9),$ hence we can apply the mean value theorem. \n\nSo, the mean value theorem states that there exists a $c\\in (4,9)$ such that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $a=4$ and $b=9$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(9)-f(4)}{9-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{(5\\sqrt{9}-2)-(5\\sqrt{4}-2)}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{13-8}{5} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5\\sqrt{x}-2)=\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{c}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{c}} & =1 \\\\ \\sqrt{c} & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ c & ={(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2} \\\\ c & =\\frac{25}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318968 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8b1a094-335a-586d-8c3d-03c43fed7e31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318224}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8b1a094-335a-586d-8c3d-03c43fed7e31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318224} :answer-field/id #uuid "26fca3f8-096e-5758-9d4d-a36cc9cf460a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318502}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e95a4d25-6c9d-57b1-8d08-3197c0c476a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-345094" :question/problem "The charge $q(t)$ on the capacitor, in coulombs, in a series RCL circuit at time $t$ seconds is\n\n$$\nq(t)={e}^{-2t}-3{e}^{-5t}+\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{4}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nFind, in amperes, the exact amplitude of the steady-state **current** $I$ in this circuit.\n\nThe amplitude of the steady-state current is\n{{field-1}}\namperes." :question/worked-solution "The current $I$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I,$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}({e}^{-2t}-3{e}^{-5t}+\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{4}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-2t}+15{e}^{-5t}-\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\frac{8}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=-2{e}^{-2t}+15{e}^{-5t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\frac{8}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\frac{8}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{6}{5})}^{2}+{(\\frac{8}{5})}^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{36+64}{25}}=2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318969 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79fa1d8b-57fa-5cc0-8d8b-c38952b89a85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317978}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79fa1d8b-57fa-5cc0-8d8b-c38952b89a85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317978} :answer-field/id #uuid "45c980fd-df74-51c9-9b6d-5d9f5a80923c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318574}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e99f439d-ed1a-5653-bd3b-a57694b6cd74" :question/math-academy-id "q-345095" :question/problem "The charge $q(t)$ on the capacitor, in coulombs, in a series RCL circuit at time $t$ seconds is\n\n$$\nq(t)=-2{e}^{-t}+{e}^{-3t}+\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{12}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFind, in amperes, the exact amplitude of the steady-state **current** $I$ in this circuit.\n\nThe amplitude of the steady-state current is\n{{field-1}}\namperes." :question/worked-solution "The current $I$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I,$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(-2{e}^{-t}+{e}^{-3t}+\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{12}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =2{e}^{-t}-3{e}^{-3t}-\\frac{10}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{24}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=2{e}^{-t}-3{e}^{-3t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{10}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{24}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{10}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{24}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{10}{13})}^{2}+{(\\frac{24}{13})}^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{676}{169}}=2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318970 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90513a9a-0af3-5328-8ab7-114c3064041c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{65}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318056}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90513a9a-0af3-5328-8ab7-114c3064041c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{65}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318056} :answer-field/id #uuid "473aef29-5766-5445-bef5-bc608ba98aae" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318578}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6d2c69af-dd17-5c0b-85fa-4c79b0a97b43" :question/math-academy-id "q-345096" :question/problem "The charge $q(t)$ on the capacitor, in coulombs, in a series RCL circuit at time $t$ seconds is\n\n$$\nq(t)=4{e}^{-2t}-{e}^{-6t}+\\frac{12}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-\\frac{5}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nFind, in amperes, the exact amplitude of the steady-state **current** $I$ in this circuit.\n\nThe amplitude of the steady-state current is\n{{field-1}}\namperes." :question/worked-solution "The current $I$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I,$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(4{e}^{-2t}-{e}^{-6t}+\\frac{12}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-\\frac{5}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)) \\\\  & =-8{e}^{-2t}+6{e}^{-6t}-\\frac{60}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)-\\frac{25}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=-8{e}^{-2t}+6{e}^{-6t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{60}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)-\\frac{25}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{60}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)-\\frac{25}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{60}{17})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{25}{17})}^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{4225}{289}}=\\frac{65}{17}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318971 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19366fe9-5cb5-5d74-8229-9ddc82d7e231" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317645} {:answer/id #uuid "5fad177d-ab0e-56ba-aac5-91728ae3feb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317889} {:answer/id #uuid "65f4293c-6240-5728-bc32-ca1ad78a1a9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317915} {:answer/id #uuid "cf2e6291-0fd9-52b0-aa52-c792c6106a4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{1}{5}q=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318251} {:answer/id #uuid "d9070aa8-aeb3-5412-87e8-3c47d66ddf3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318286}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19366fe9-5cb5-5d74-8229-9ddc82d7e231" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317645} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5f796f5-07e9-529f-b979-d850b75e709b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318851}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a5536751-fcf7-5c91-932b-4c365b86809a" :question/math-academy-id "q-345103" :question/problem "A series RCL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $24\\,Ω,$ a capacitance of $0.05\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductance of $L=4\\,\\text{H}.$ Find the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds).\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "We have the following circuit diagram.\n\n![](images/64/6494f1905ab90cb3d5655aaa824726255dc7332841ec656dc88c2180b0f93a1c.png)\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=24\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=24I=24\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.05}=20q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=4\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=4\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)=24\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+20q+4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318972 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14202c3b-6dd7-571b-9808-383358e8a74b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317629} {:answer/id #uuid "2197e1f1-471a-5deb-84cc-a47e49dbd8ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317669} {:answer/id #uuid "66379b2b-956f-5f47-8f1f-dc49f671d428" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317916} {:answer/id #uuid "8f0289ff-daab-5172-b33a-55d0dd592202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318052} {:answer/id #uuid "9fb2eef1-8fcd-5e28-896a-c914b03421a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{1}{2}q=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318110}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14202c3b-6dd7-571b-9808-383358e8a74b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317629} :answer-field/id #uuid "535ecfbb-6cf7-54de-9e20-0f850f747224" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318604}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4d7efbe4-b551-57a3-9a49-0e74c5bd54a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-345105" :question/problem "A series RCL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $15\\,Ω,$ a capacitance of $0.1\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductance of $L=5\\,\\text{H}.$ Find the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds).\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "We have the following circuit diagram.\n\n![](images/a0/a051a6380df51ba3c1141bc69952ba442dc9c816df30c5640846894fe1ba4c31.png)\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=15\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=15I=15\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.1}=10q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=5\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=5\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)=15\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q+5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318973 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61e375a5-f277-57f3-8646-828261041c89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+8q=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317897} {:answer/id #uuid "aae0672e-049d-5646-b127-2a9bb8b25db7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+0.08q=15\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318145} {:answer/id #uuid "da55299f-7ee9-5d92-a8e8-5e1072cbc8e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+8q=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318294} {:answer/id #uuid "e3cdd000-21b7-5eb2-bfe4-8dec1512f2f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+8q=15\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318323} {:answer/id #uuid "faf7c3de-ed9a-5cea-b3cf-aecb791a082c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+0.08q=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318402}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da55299f-7ee9-5d92-a8e8-5e1072cbc8e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+8q=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318294} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e44f905-64d1-5dbe-9867-8e47757a60c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318691}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c75c18d9-d88e-5fd0-b738-dee782dbc767" :question/math-academy-id "q-345112" :question/problem "![](images/a7/a73d5f0c60062001e947301760fe1cdae7e0e17c7fcc9870baf9d0262070b238.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $15\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $20\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.025\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $5\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=20\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=20I=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.025}=40q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=5\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=5\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n15\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+40q+5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+8q=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318974 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "083e53c2-e293-5e95-afb7-f240120d0b98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317598} {:answer/id #uuid "9e2a45df-02e7-5ddb-ba89-f493b05e3245" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318103} {:answer/id #uuid "adbca0b4-9d48-5b6f-b01e-f211f322c0d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+3q=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318155} {:answer/id #uuid "ee994e39-93a9-5c06-9e92-41fe7aeed852" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318363} {:answer/id #uuid "fb8e2ad9-86b3-5ff9-ad70-e36660a30bc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318405}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "083e53c2-e293-5e95-afb7-f240120d0b98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317598} :answer-field/id #uuid "e53343f7-0df7-53e3-b136-454227114d26" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318905}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "50bfaf9e-5fa3-5ce2-93c0-cd914192855f" :question/math-academy-id "q-345115" :question/problem "![](images/8c/8cc2afe50a1f1e3ed0a173cdba6f5a5f59145b0afa6038bee7c9a1fe720238a6.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $12\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $20\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.125\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $4\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=20\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=20I=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.125}=8q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=4\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=4\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n12=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+8q+4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=3.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318977 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9cd6110-ee79-54aa-b5f6-0c07b7d4580c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318185}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9cd6110-ee79-54aa-b5f6-0c07b7d4580c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318185} :answer-field/id #uuid "05247bc3-29c4-502b-be4c-6f37f3e8eb36" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318438}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8d91e5be-fcc2-5907-a8d6-f32f94f6bce2" :question/math-academy-id "q-345127" :question/problem "A series RCL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $15\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $30\\,Ω,$ a capacitance of $\\frac{1}{10}\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductance of $L=6\\,\\text{H}.$ Find the steady-state charge for this circuit.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}}\ncoulombs." :question/worked-solution "The diagram for our circuit is shown below.\n\n![](images/d7/d7145f4b394e7675b87ed851e344756ecb8441ce737f8664fbfd4b0e426ef36f.png)\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops. In an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=30\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=30I=30\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{(1/10)}=10q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=6\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=6\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=6\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=6\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n6\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+30\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=15.\n$$\n\nThe *steady-state charge* is the value that the charge $q(t)$ approaches as $t\\to \\infty .$ In a stable RCL circuit with a constant electromotive force, the transient response eventually decays to zero, leaving a constant charge.\n\nWhen the system reaches steady state, the charge $q$ is constant. Consequently, the first and second derivatives of the charge with respect to time are zero:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these steady-state conditions into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n6(0)+30(0)+10q=15,\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n10q=15\\,⟹\\,q=\\frac{15}{10}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nPhysically, this represents the state where the capacitor is fully charged and the current has ceased to flow.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is $\\frac{3}{2}$ coulombs."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318980 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04576746-22cd-5bf8-9233-21b513d957fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317582} {:answer/id #uuid "14c0b609-97a6-51fa-889b-c52b77d46fd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317632} {:answer/id #uuid "3860759b-c4e9-5d02-95ba-50ef05265e44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317746} {:answer/id #uuid "98819183-8c07-589d-aec6-c786dbb0030d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318088} {:answer/id #uuid "bc6e176a-1f8c-5941-9c51-9e41348873c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318191}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98819183-8c07-589d-aec6-c786dbb0030d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318088} :answer-field/id #uuid "063808cf-6ae1-5933-8a6f-380ed77084b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318440}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fbfa339e-c615-578f-b729-cc9c3ed650e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-345132" :question/problem "![](images/7c/7cbcdf7d43a99b9558b1d085d2306c22e1d27134a5b0ea428721ce7bd074b725.png)\n\nConsider the RCL circuit shown above. Given that the charge $q(t)$ of the capacitor at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nq(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-4t}+\\frac{P}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\frac{Q}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $A,$ $B,$ $P,$ and $Q$ are constants, find the value of $P+Q.$\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $5\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.25\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=5\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=5I=5\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.25}=4q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=1\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=1\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)=5\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+4q+\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\cdot \\frac{dq}{dt}+4q=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\cdot \\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+4{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-4t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a sinusoidal with argument $t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))+5(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\ +4(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ (3\\alpha +5\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+(-5\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3\\alpha +5\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)) \\\\ -5\\alpha +3\\beta &=2\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{5}{17},$ $\\beta =\\frac{3}{17}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{5}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\frac{3}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-4t}-\\frac{5}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\frac{3}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $P=-5,$ $Q=3,$ and\n\n$$\nP+Q=-5+3=-2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318981 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d60619b-e76d-5790-8aba-39d218afed81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317943} {:answer/id #uuid "732fdcb2-869e-554f-8854-7ab6543baf90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317961} {:answer/id #uuid "83c93c03-7f9a-5a5f-ab3c-d9ff69e42544" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.00" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318012} {:answer/id #uuid "8de5b245-4705-5de6-bf30-f81a91441578" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.71" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318046} {:answer/id #uuid "d48830f4-0bc6-54ba-a8e9-bc11390ebdbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.86" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318272}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8de5b245-4705-5de6-bf30-f81a91441578" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.71" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318046} :answer-field/id #uuid "25fb76b0-9b9a-5e94-af61-d4768b49b60d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318499}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f781abc5-81a3-5e42-a46f-9bd991ddcdd0" :question/math-academy-id "q-345135" :question/problem "![](images/11/1146917884634beea990c281a591257898887a11029f0116e10f8bfd38169282.png)\n\nFind, in coulombs, the amplitude of the steady state charge for the RCL circuit shown above, rounded to two decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $(10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $15\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.1\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $5\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=15\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=15I=15\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.1}=10q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=5\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=5\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)=15\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q+5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\cdot \\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\cdot \\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+2{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-2t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a sinusoidal with argument $t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))+3(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\, &  \\\\ +2(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ (-3\\alpha +\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+(\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha +\\beta &=2\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)) \\\\ \\alpha +3\\beta &=-1\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{7}{10},$ $\\beta =-\\frac{1}{10}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{7}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-2t}-\\frac{7}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-2t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{7}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)≈{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{7}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{7}{10})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}}≈0.71,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318983 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "006fab69-d6f3-56c8-8155-a3e19388f4f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317571} {:answer/id #uuid "3e841afd-07d0-5f8c-8863-bb24e07697a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.58" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317764} {:answer/id #uuid "64ff1ba2-fd7e-5c02-ba32-921ff68c9177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317912} {:answer/id #uuid "981fa6db-af4b-5154-8495-3b9f55facab6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318084} {:answer/id #uuid "e0b42a27-633b-52a5-ba1c-e5e20732caf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318314}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64ff1ba2-fd7e-5c02-ba32-921ff68c9177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317912} :answer-field/id #uuid "4665862c-886b-5080-9686-2600e000dfbc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318577}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3c27e0ae-78a1-5371-b073-4bc4c4aae1d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-345144" :question/problem "![](images/42/42ab81638355355340008eaa54657a0144612ec9d04e44266350cdb8012051de.png)\n\nFind, in coulombs, the amplitude of the steady state charge for the RCL circuit shown above, rounded to two decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $(8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-41\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $30\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.05\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $5\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=30\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=30I=30\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.05}=20q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=5\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=5\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-41\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)=30\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+20q+5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\cdot \\frac{dq}{dt}+4q=\\frac{8}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{41}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\cdot \\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+4{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{(-3+\\sqrt{5})t}+B{e}^{(-3-\\sqrt{5})t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))+6(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\, &  \\\\ +4(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =\\frac{8}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{41}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-5\\alpha +18\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+(-18\\alpha -5\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =\\frac{8}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{41}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-5\\alpha +18\\beta &=\\frac{8}{5}\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\ -18\\alpha -5\\beta &=-\\frac{41}{5}\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{2}{5},$ $\\beta =\\frac{1}{5}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{(-3+\\sqrt{5})t}+B{e}^{(-3-\\sqrt{5})t}+\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{(-3+\\sqrt{5})t}+B{e}^{(-3-\\sqrt{5})t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)≈{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}}≈0.45,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318990 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16a5bdeb-b514-5d0e-aaf0-7bec315700a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{(t+1)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317637}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16a5bdeb-b514-5d0e-aaf0-7bec315700a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{(t+1)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317637} :answer-field/id #uuid "8dad6dfd-8e84-57dc-8946-f16fcfd476e8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318737}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8020a70a-eeac-5c07-988e-4b374888b493" :question/math-academy-id "q-345209" :question/problem "A body of mass $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{kg}$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.  At time $t$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=\\frac{2t}{t+1}$ meters per second.  Find an expression for the force acting on the particle at time $t.$\n\n$F(t)=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "By Newton’s second law,\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2t}{t+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(t+1)\\cdot (2t)^{′}-2t\\cdot (t+1)^{′}}{(t+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(t+1)-2t(1)}{(t+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2t+2)-2t}{(t+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(t+1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =ma(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{(t+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(t+1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318991 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "300e195c-ddeb-57cf-a4bf-c0e974317b20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317710}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "300e195c-ddeb-57cf-a4bf-c0e974317b20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317710} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b62001e-c670-5582-880c-5e2e4169c1ea" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318546}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "71f329b9-d17b-5f51-b752-6c65f97ea24c" :question/math-academy-id "q-345223" :question/problem "A body of mass $\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}).$  At time $t$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ meters per second. \n\nCalculate the magnitude of the force at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ seconds. Do not round the answer.\n\nThe magnitude of the force equals\n{{field-1}} $\\text{N}.$" :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law,\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the acceleration at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,\\text{s}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =ma(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the magnitude of the force is\n\n$$\n|F(\\frac{\\pi }{4})|=|2|=2\\,\\text{N}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319004 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39bb6fd0-ab83-5955-9da3-4caab22d47c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317751}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39bb6fd0-ab83-5955-9da3-4caab22d47c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317751} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9cb0d01-55ea-5aaa-a85d-5bfb232facd4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a4258571-e2ee-5711-b11c-465f2f2d9827" :question/math-academy-id "q-345321" :question/problem "![](images/21/2102626f331aaa7803462f8e427ef2c0150deb554955199f3d402123934e815a.png)\n\nA region is bounded by the curve $y={x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+3$ and the $x$-axis between $x=-1$ and $x=1$, as shown above. Find the area $A$ of the region. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the area, we just need to compute the corresponding definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-2\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+3x){|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{2}{3}+3)-(\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{2}{3}-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{31}{12}-(-\\frac{25}{12}) \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319008 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ee21fdb-4f31-5662-8173-652df2970035" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{t}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317766}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ee21fdb-4f31-5662-8173-652df2970035" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{t}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317766} :answer-field/id #uuid "43d3cb79-dfa8-57a8-8ad1-d8a7c894979b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318569}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4a5942b5-b345-55e9-98fa-6a0c078bad31" :question/math-academy-id "q-345340" :question/problem "A body of mass $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.  At time $t$ seconds, the position of the particle is given by $s(t)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(t)$ meters.  Find the expression for the force acting on the particle at time $t.$\n\n$F(t)=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "By Newton’s second law,\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[{({t}^{2}+1)}^{-1}] \\\\  & =-{({t}^{2}+1)}^{-2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}\\cdot 2t \\\\  & =-\\frac{2t}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =ma(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot [-\\frac{2t}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{t}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319011 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "418f9337-5384-52da-981d-31d9661f516d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317777}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "418f9337-5384-52da-981d-31d9661f516d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317777} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbf3ec70-b28b-5016-ba4e-0a5a05b82943" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318858}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "af180c34-7e0c-5b7b-9e5b-5a6251599f15" :question/math-academy-id "q-345381" :question/problem "![](images/dc/dce89dbea62566c0ee897bffe0638458970799bafdc7d7c8e5084328fef38bef.png)\n\nA region is bounded by the curve $y=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}$, the $x$-axis, and the lines $x=-1$ and $x=1$, as shown above. Find the area $A$ of the region. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the area, we just need to compute the corresponding definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-arcsin\\,(\\frac{x}{2}){|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =-(arcsin\\,(\\frac{1}{2})-arcsin\\,(-\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+(-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we obtained a negative number. So the area that we want is \n\n$$\nA=-{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319013 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62a7906c-2a37-5849-b484-c4001173664d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317901}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62a7906c-2a37-5849-b484-c4001173664d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317901} :answer-field/id #uuid "1cba2d9b-a343-5b67-885f-6b596a66cb05" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318486}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ac6cc325-8109-5df6-a8fa-f84e9e1ea235" :question/math-academy-id "q-345385" :question/problem "![](images/d4/d43d7419f631b479478641ac203c6816693c68ffc40ccc1d4a8a72da0a03461a.png)\n\nA region is bounded by the curve $y=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}}}$, the $x$-axis, and the lines $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{3}{2}$, as shown above. Find the area $A$ of the region. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the area, we just need to compute the corresponding definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3/2}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =arccos\\,(\\frac{x}{3}){|}_{0}^{3/2} \\\\  & =arccos\\,(\\frac{1}{2})-arccos\\,(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we obtained a negative number. So the area that we want is \n\n$$\nA=-{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319024 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "193fe597-715a-5d01-af1c-71fb91a8c26d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317646}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "193fe597-715a-5d01-af1c-71fb91a8c26d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317646} :answer-field/id #uuid "4861563e-97fd-5b25-8865-eb95ee9e7cf1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318583} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "daf8cfa9-5ea5-5eb9-b8b4-6187321a94fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318297}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "daf8cfa9-5ea5-5eb9-b8b4-6187321a94fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318297} :answer-field/id #uuid "7291b310-c1df-5538-adaf-946d11b24c06" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318669}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bc894b99-a438-5f57-9099-db4cf3a5ce6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-345502" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ In **ascending order**, the **exact values** of the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =arctan\\,(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})+n\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$\n\n- setting $n=-1$ gives $x=-\\frac{5\\pi }{6},$\n\n- setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$ So, the solutions to this equation for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\,x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\,\\text{and}\\,x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319064 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2aace116-779e-5cd7-87fe-2a773103e41b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317697}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2aace116-779e-5cd7-87fe-2a773103e41b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317697} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1fe8d6b-b674-5235-b645-ef3a0cb2483c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318841}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "af8d7c5c-930c-59d3-b49e-ff7666f63383" :question/math-academy-id "q-346081" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{4})\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=\\frac{\\pi x}{4},$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\pi /2$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can rewrite the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{4})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{4}{\\pi }\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319065 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60f9d185-78b6-59cc-a36a-404564ab8739" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317893}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60f9d185-78b6-59cc-a36a-404564ab8739" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317893} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b52d148-4c11-50af-96f7-2d509700ed26" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318545}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "58a433e2-084f-559f-bd64-1862ef8ffdc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-346084" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =0-(-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319066 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5ed63f3-57cc-59c7-b8f7-81f7530ff3ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318217}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5ed63f3-57cc-59c7-b8f7-81f7530ff3ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318217} :answer-field/id #uuid "f305be5a-3143-5d6a-b028-5ceb30c2d005" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318928}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8ad5354f-415f-5373-a369-188a90efa535" :question/math-academy-id "q-346090" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}(-{csc}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}(-{csc}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}(-{csc}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,{|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =cot\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-cot\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}-\\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-3}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319067 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd55a046-ddb4-56de-ab65-a51210d86e05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318246}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd55a046-ddb4-56de-ab65-a51210d86e05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318246} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa47e58e-1210-5f7d-ae4f-2c6efa1bc040" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318948}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f3de3131-f650-58c5-a909-7a85b83b3f4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-346092" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-(-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}+1 \\\\  & =1-\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319068 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "769b09c3-8e5a-5e55-99cd-e126e57155aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}-2}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317972}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "769b09c3-8e5a-5e55-99cd-e126e57155aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}-2}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317972} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ff5d4bc-02ea-5674-92d8-9925921dbed3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318597}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a00832dd-966d-5a5a-8da4-9f3ee1f87bfb" :question/math-academy-id "q-346094" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\,{|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-(-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}-(-2) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}-2}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319070 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca8bcc6d-5e6e-5f23-9931-9ba109153e2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2i}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318235}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca8bcc6d-5e6e-5f23-9931-9ba109153e2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2i}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318235} :answer-field/id #uuid "0de184e0-4c2b-55bb-bae9-b8061c599b03" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318456} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f64ae7e0-64b1-5f21-9e91-7ee063a78db3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2i}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318378}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f64ae7e0-64b1-5f21-9e91-7ee063a78db3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2i}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318378} :answer-field/id #uuid "92bf9fa3-b3c5-557c-b2c0-e0fba9edb465" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318747}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "18197a01-d08b-5472-bcd0-342b90f62fdf" :question/math-academy-id "q-346179" :question/problem "Express ${\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{3}+2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x$ as the limit of a left Riemann sum.\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{3}+2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[(($\n{{field-1}}\n$)^{3}+2($\n{{field-2}}\n$)+1)\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]$" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{3}+2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)={x}^{3}+2x+1,$ the lower bound is $a=0,$ and the upper bound is $b=2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-0}{n}=\\frac{2}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{3}+2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(0+i\\cdot \\frac{2}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(\\frac{2i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[({(\\,\\frac{2i}{n}\\,)}^{3}+2(\\,\\frac{2i}{n}\\,)+1)\\,\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319072 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33e27707-d1a6-54e9-95db-6c0e39ed6676" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317731}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33e27707-d1a6-54e9-95db-6c0e39ed6676" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317731} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d25b445-440a-5f14-80c7-7f5d50d92fbe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318487}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e8c498fe-e615-5851-9e66-78de56e7b50e" :question/math-academy-id "q-346181" :question/problem "Given that ${\\int }_{6}^{-3}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{113}{9},$ calculate ${\\int }_{-3}^{6}(9f(x)+8x)\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\n${\\int }_{-3}^{6}(9f(x)+8x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{6}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{6}^{-3}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{113}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can split up the given integral using the sum rule and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{6}(9f(x)+8x)\\,\\text{d}x & =9{\\int }_{-3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,+\\,8{\\int }_{-3}^{6}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =9(-\\frac{113}{9})\\,+\\,8\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,{|}_{-3}^{6} \\\\  & =-113\\,+\\,8\\cdot \\frac{{6}^{2}-(-3)^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-113\\,+\\,8\\cdot \\frac{27}{2} \\\\  & =-113\\,+\\,108 \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319085 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b3a545a-f6fc-56d8-b618-306dd6c9b24f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{3i}{n}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317656}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b3a545a-f6fc-56d8-b618-306dd6c9b24f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{3i}{n}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317656} :answer-field/id #uuid "2169b17d-9b76-5e27-8f32-b49b8393f666" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318495}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "76bb0795-0840-56ba-91a7-84e2ebc07a76" :question/math-academy-id "q-346217" :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]$" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\sqrt{x+1},$ the lower bound is $a=0$ and the upper bound is $b=3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-0}{n}=\\frac{3}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(0+i\\cdot \\frac{3}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(\\frac{3i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{(\\frac{3i}{n})+1}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{3i}{n}+1}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319089 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "869b8c29-ad99-5c1d-bdd5-cb1ad192a214" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318022}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "869b8c29-ad99-5c1d-bdd5-cb1ad192a214" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318022} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ee5e5c7-596a-5614-bb03-ce5ccba93bfb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318694}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "75f6ce5d-5587-5359-99a4-e02f7c9a6617" :question/math-academy-id "q-346240" :question/problem "A contractor has $18\\,in$ of trim to mark out a rectangular opening. Find the maximum area ${A}_{\\text{max}}$ that can be marked out with the trim. Do not round the answer.\n\n${A}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,{in}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1**: Let us call $x$ and $y$ the dimensions of the rectagular area.\n\n![](images/59/59c6b4e09015898c1cd77d96fba2bc0dca037ef7462a15f90765257888e3331d.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We need to find the values of $x$ and $y$ such as the area\n\n$$\nA=xy\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 3**: This is subject to the constraint that the perimeter is $18.$ This gives the equation\n\n$$\n2x+2y=18.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We make one of the variables the subject in the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+2y & =18 \\\\ y & =9-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y=9-x$ in the expression $A=xy$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =x(9-x) \\\\ A & =9x-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation ${A}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(x)=9-2x,\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 9-2x & =0 \\\\ 2x & =9 \\\\ x & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $x=\\frac{9}{2}$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{A}^{″}(x)=-2\\,⟹\\,{A}^{″}(\\frac{9}{2})=-2<0.\n$$\n\nSince ${A}^{″}(\\frac{9}{2})$ is negative, we conclude that $x=\\frac{9}{2}$ is indeed a maximum of $A(x).$\n\nFinally, when $x=\\frac{9}{2}$, we have \n\n$$\ny=9-\\frac{9}{2}=\\frac{9}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the rectangle with the largest area are $x=\\frac{9}{2}\\,in$ and $y=\\frac{9}{2}\\,in,$ and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{max}}=xy=\\frac{9}{2}\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}=20.25\\,{in}^{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319092 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c49f865-99de-519b-9b0e-6f73589c8822" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317611}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c49f865-99de-519b-9b0e-6f73589c8822" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317611} :answer-field/id #uuid "f048a17c-f1b2-56a1-8a31-975762ef6ad9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318923}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f33e4009-fc1f-57ea-a385-f971677ad6e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-346255" :question/problem "A farmer wants to build a rectangular livestock pen of area $648\\,{ft}^{2}.$ One side of the pen is adjacent to a barn, so that side does not require fencing. Find $x+y$ where $x$ and $y$ are the dimensions of the pen that minimize the amount of fencing required.\n\n$x+y=$\n{{field-1}} $\\text{ft}$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1**: We draw a diagram, and introduce variables for the length and width of the fencing.\n\n![](images/83/8377f61340020a602e6226932a14fb56ceb803bc9efff05223682f9cca1cf91e.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We need to find the *minimum* value of the perimeter\n\n$$\nP=x+2y.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: The constraint is that the area is exactly $648\\,{ft}^{2}.$ This gives the equation\n\n$$\nxy=648.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Solving the above constraint equation for $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{648}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into our expression for $P$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =x+2(\\frac{648}{x}) \\\\  & =x+\\frac{1296}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation ${P}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=1-\\frac{1296}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{1296}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1296 \\\\ x & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $x=36$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=\\frac{2592}{{x}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{P}^{″}(36)=\\frac{2592}{(36)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{18}>0\\,.\n$$\n\nSince ${P}^{″}(36)$ is positive, we conclude that $x=36$ is indeed a local minimum of the function $P(x).$\n\nFinally, when $x=36,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{648}{36}=18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the lot that has the least perimeter are $x=36\\,ft$ and $y=18\\,ft,$ and we conclude that \n\n$$\nx+y=36+18=54\\,ft.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319093 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bef11467-f6f1-5099-a450-87b5379d2b15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318198}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bef11467-f6f1-5099-a450-87b5379d2b15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318198} :answer-field/id #uuid "8be0ff7c-611b-584d-b02c-0798a35d84aa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318730}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "970cce65-2ceb-5bbc-b96e-29b0275ee6d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-346256" :question/problem "A park wants to build a rectangular skating area of area $1250\\,{yd}^{2}.$ One side of the area is adjacent to a wall, so that side does not require fencing. Find $x+y$ where $x$ and $y$ are the dimensions of the area that minimize the amount of fencing required.\n\n$x+y=$\n{{field-1}} $\\text{yd}$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1**: We draw a diagram, and introduce variables for the length and width of the fencing.\n\n![](images/f1/f1ce707b311c320fe7dbf3ba2ad1daff6669a67ab1d7652fc8d98065c9ae747d.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We need to find the *minimum* value of the perimeter\n\n$$\nP=x+2y.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: The constraint is that the area is exactly $1250\\,{yd}^{2}.$ This gives the equation\n\n$$\nxy=1250.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Solving the above constraint equation for $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1250}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into our expression for $P$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =x+2(\\frac{1250}{x}) \\\\  & =x+\\frac{2500}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation ${P}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=1-\\frac{2500}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{2500}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =2500 \\\\ x & =50.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $x=50$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=\\frac{5000}{{x}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{P}^{″}(50)=\\frac{5000}{(50)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{25}>0\\,.\n$$\n\nSince ${P}^{″}(50)$ is positive, we conclude that $x=50$ is indeed a local minimum of the function $P(x).$\n\nFinally, when $x=50,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1250}{50}=25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the lot that has the least perimeter are $x=50\\,yd$ and $y=25\\,yd,$ and we conclude that \n\n$$\nx+y=50+25=75\\,yd.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319094 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5eb82788-efbe-50de-b226-6bde5d6aedf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317886}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5eb82788-efbe-50de-b226-6bde5d6aedf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317886} :answer-field/id #uuid "47ffe2b0-f398-575b-afe8-d12d64c3f4fc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318580}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ec7ce12f-cbf4-5c03-8053-383589db4d45" :question/math-academy-id "q-346368" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{2\\pi /3}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=\\frac{x}{4},$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=4\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $2\\pi /3$ | $2\\pi $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\pi /6$ | $\\pi /2$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can rewrite the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2\\pi /3}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(u)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(u)\\cdot 4\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(u)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4(-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(u))\\,{|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =4(-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})) \\\\  & =4(-1+2) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319105 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a18383d3-63cd-5639-931d-a7a1c504ecad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318118} {:answer/id #uuid "eef5da2c-2eb9-5619-b5fc-8c0b83845f3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527772}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eef5da2c-2eb9-5619-b5fc-8c0b83845f3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527772} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4db02f7-5977-595a-8560-ebd6520e3bc1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318876}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "684a7fc3-f685-5be8-90e1-52ef15ddd822" :question/math-academy-id "q-346458" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{5}\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{100-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{5}\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{100-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =6{\\int }_{0}^{5}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{100\\cdot (1-{x}^{2}/100)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{10}{\\int }_{0}^{5}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}/100}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{5}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/10)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{10})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{10}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{10}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=10\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{5}\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{100-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{5}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/10)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot 10\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u\\,{|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})-6\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0) \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319109 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "578ea0f9-6e27-559f-8d20-f7994570e05b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317858}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "578ea0f9-6e27-559f-8d20-f7994570e05b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317858} :answer-field/id #uuid "35bbb5d1-0990-5ae6-9af4-dfc7a684e8ee" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318537} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "284de721-bcb0-5584-98b1-7d0cec8bc940" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317690}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "284de721-bcb0-5584-98b1-7d0cec8bc940" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317690} :answer-field/id #uuid "9565b1c0-a7b6-54b9-9567-456ae91899dd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318753}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "198c9f13-854c-5a78-a110-5897652614aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-346519" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{20}{25-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$artanh\\,(${{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "$$\n\nNote that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{20}{25-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{4}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{x}{5})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{artanh}⁡u+C,\n$$\n\nthe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{5})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{5}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{5}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=5\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{3}{5}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{20}{25-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{4}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{x}{5})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{3/5}\\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\cdot 5\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{3/5}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{1-{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{artanh}⁡u\\,{|}_{0}^{3/5} \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{3}{5})-4\\operatorname{artanh}⁡0 \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{3}{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319110 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f286994-9b4f-5dee-b8a9-1575e6938302" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}(2{e}^{3}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318106}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f286994-9b4f-5dee-b8a9-1575e6938302" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}(2{e}^{3}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318106} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a98dba4-54f8-5f32-8ce5-f4039b42cddc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318449}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4b4d889c-3125-509c-8bd0-37836a63b86f" :question/math-academy-id "q-346521" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1/4}^{{e}^{3}/4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1/4}^{{e}^{3}/4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{1/4}^{{e}^{3}/4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4x\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x)\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {v}^{′}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int 1\\cdot \\,dx=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/4}^{{e}^{3}/4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{{e}^{3}/4}u{v}^{′}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv{|}_{1/4}^{{e}^{3}/4}-{\\int }_{1/4}^{{e}^{3}/4}v{u}^{′}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x)\\,(x){|}_{1/4}^{{e}^{3}/4}-{\\int }_{1/4}^{{e}^{3}/4}x(\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{3}}{4})\\,(\\frac{{e}^{3}}{4})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4})\\,(\\frac{1}{4})]-{\\int }_{1/4}^{{e}^{3}/4}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{3})-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)-x{|}_{1/4}^{{e}^{3}/4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{3}-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{3}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(2{e}^{3}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319111 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bdf4bb63-02ff-5ebf-8e66-c6be8c85edd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\operatorname{ln}(2+\\sqrt{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318195}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bdf4bb63-02ff-5ebf-8e66-c6be8c85edd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\operatorname{ln}(2+\\sqrt{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318195} :answer-field/id #uuid "64001866-4276-554f-984c-71378cda4ad0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318639}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6f5d1a83-72d6-5baf-8989-3f4b0012df34" :question/math-academy-id "q-346522" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9}}\\,\\text{d}x=4{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse hyperbolic sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{3}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=3\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9}}\\,\\text{d}x & =4{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}}\\cdot (3\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =12{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}} \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡u{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡2-12\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡0 \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡2 \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+\\sqrt{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319118 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af226b09-d827-5c1c-aaf8-a979730d39f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318157}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af226b09-d827-5c1c-aaf8-a979730d39f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318157} :answer-field/id #uuid "f15b0103-0a30-53b1-b24e-a7de7389c80b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318925}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9846049f-0423-59a8-8569-7eab74f54e97" :question/math-academy-id "q-346555" :question/problem "Calculate $F\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ where $F(x)={\\int }_{0}^{x}4{sec}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.$\n\n$F\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $F(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\nF\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4})={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}4{sec}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nThen, computing the definite integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}4{sec}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =4(1-0) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319120 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0626dc1a-6232-5b90-9385-e5ce50b107d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15+\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317590}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0626dc1a-6232-5b90-9385-e5ce50b107d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15+\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317590} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb73beaa-867a-55f5-abae-114501789a36" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318950}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c755b337-b4d4-5ea7-9e4c-878004ac0d92" :question/math-academy-id "q-346571" :question/problem "Thermal energy is being added to a device at a rate of $3t$ joules per second. If at time $t=0$ the device already contains $15$ joules of energy, write an expression for $E(t),$ the total energy in the device at time $t,$ where $t$ is measured in seconds.\n\n$E(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(t)=3t$ represent the rate at which energy enters the device, and let $E(T)$ be the total energy in the device after $T$ seconds.\n\nUsing our formula for the accumulation of change, we have\n\n$$\nE(T)=E(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E(T) & =E(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =15+{\\int }_{0}^{T}3t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={15+(\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{2})|}_{0}^{T} \\\\  & =15+\\frac{3{T}^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's rewrite our final answer in terms of the original variable  $t.$ We have\n\n$$\nE(t)=15+\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319121 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd785ee6-6715-53f7-8cdf-ba030fe99857" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318306}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd785ee6-6715-53f7-8cdf-ba030fe99857" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318306} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fb330fb-3336-561a-98fa-cec7e2718d25" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318698}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2bb34ddd-c2ee-57af-a0a2-9fb98a895f33" :question/math-academy-id "q-346575" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}(1-{cot}^{2}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}))\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "From the identity $1+{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta ={csc}^{2}⁡\\theta $ with $\\theta =x-\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{cot}^{2}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & ={csc}^{2}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\ {cot}^{2}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & ={csc}^{2}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}(1-{cot}^{2}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}))\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}(1-{csc}^{2}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}(2-{csc}^{2}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}2\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}{csc}^{2}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2x\\,{|}_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}+\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,{|}_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{6})-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =2(\\frac{\\pi }{6})+\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}-\\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319122 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a96d98f9-09fc-51b5-a09c-4c2fde0fa6fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318141}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a96d98f9-09fc-51b5-a09c-4c2fde0fa6fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318141} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bb74c53-51d2-5629-8fd0-821887d13160" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318729}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b23fa132-091d-593b-a542-6eca17f50321" :question/math-academy-id "q-346576" :question/problem "The total number of pages of a document printed $t$ minutes after printing begins increases at a rate of $\\frac{18}{(t+1)^{2}}.$ If, at $t=0$ minutes, the printer had already produced $40$ pages, how many pages will the printer have produced after $2$ minutes?\n\nAfter $2$ minutes the printer has produced\n{{field-1}} pages." :question/worked-solution "Let $a(t)=\\frac{18}{(t+1)^{2}}$ be the rate at which the number of pages produced is changing, and let $A(T)$ be the number of pages produced $T$ minutes after printing begins. \n\nUsing our formula for the accumulation of change, we have\n\n$$\nA(T)=A(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}a(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(T) & =A(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =40+{\\int }_{0}^{T}\\frac{18}{(t+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =40+(-\\frac{18}{t+1}){|}_{0}^{T} \\\\  & =40+(-\\frac{18}{T+1})-(-\\frac{18}{1}) \\\\  & =40-\\frac{18}{T+1}+18 \\\\  & =58-\\frac{18}{T+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the number of pages produced $T=2$ minutes after printing begins, we substitute in the above:\n\n$$\nA(2)=40-\\frac{18}{2+1}+18=52.\n$$\n\nThus, after $2$ minutes, the printer has produced $52$ pages."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319132 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cc8ab50-9678-5fe2-a058-74e9262eb4f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317660}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1cc8ab50-9678-5fe2-a058-74e9262eb4f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317660} :answer-field/id #uuid "b596f4a3-fa7b-57e3-b7e2-1fe01583da6e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318814}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6f3ba61f-934e-53ed-a21e-8c527c008d63" :question/math-academy-id "q-346702" :question/problem "![](images/78/7808295803d04d2c996ca7d36110f070a60f4491107ad1c831e6fa8ea9f85678.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ of the finite region bounded between the curve $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)$ and the $y$-axis, as shown above.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the $y$-intercepts of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y) \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)} & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\pi y) & =1 \\\\ \\pi y & =…,-\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{5\\pi }{4},… \\\\ y & =…,-\\frac{3}{4},\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{5}{4},…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that we want to find the area between the curve $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)$ and the $y$-axis from $y=-\\frac{3}{4}$ to $y=\\frac{1}{4}.$ To find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3/4}^{1/4}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y))\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3/4}^{1/4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{-3/4}^{1/4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)}{\\pi }|}_{-3/4}^{1/4}-{\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)}{\\pi }|}_{-3/4}^{1/4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\pi }[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})]-\\frac{1}{\\pi }[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\pi }[\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})]-\\frac{1}{\\pi }[\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})] \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\pi }-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtained a negative number because the region lies to the left of the $y$-axis. So the area that we want is\n\n$$\nA=|-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\pi }|=\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\pi }.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319133 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da371f43-276c-5b18-9da5-af0ba94cea69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}(\\frac{2}{3})+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318292}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da371f43-276c-5b18-9da5-af0ba94cea69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}(\\frac{2}{3})+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318292} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d176162-d1f8-59b4-b435-50afc62c79e0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318767}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6f963953-4ca0-5b72-8632-5bdad8051d11" :question/math-academy-id "q-346710" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{x+2}{(x-4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+2}{(x-4)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x-4}+\\frac{B}{(x-4)^{2}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ and $B,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x-4)^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\nx+2=A(x-4)+B.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=4,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4+2 & =A(4-4)+B \\\\ B & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $B,$ we can solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+2 & =A(0-4)+6 \\\\ 2 & =-4A+6 \\\\ 4A & =4 \\\\ A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+2}{(x-4)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{x-4}+\\frac{6}{(x-4)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{x+2}{(x-4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{1}{x-4}+\\frac{6}{(x-4)^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x+6{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{(x-4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|\\,{|}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{6}{x-4}\\,{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2-4|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1-4|)-(\\frac{6}{2-4}-\\frac{6}{1-4}) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)-(-3-(-2)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319137 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d279eda-667c-555e-8386-f505329564d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}3-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317986}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d279eda-667c-555e-8386-f505329564d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}3-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317986} :answer-field/id #uuid "30bd3f50-8128-5d2f-a7f6-94157ad1e225" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318523}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "68d214ab-9577-5e8b-9200-4e23d1e48429" :question/math-academy-id "q-346752" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{x-4}{{x}^{2}-2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's first factor the denominator of the integrand:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-4}{{x}^{2}-2x+1}=\\frac{x-4}{(x-1)^{2}}\n$$\n\nWe must convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-4}{(x-1)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x-1}+\\frac{B}{(x-1)^{2}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A$ and $B,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x-1)^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\nx-4=A(x-1)+B.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=1,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-4 & =A(1-1)+B \\\\ B & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $B,$ we can solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0-4 & =A(0-1)+B \\\\ -4 & =-A-3 \\\\ A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-4}{(x-1)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{x-1}-\\frac{3}{(x-1)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{x-4}{(x-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}(\\frac{1}{x-1}-\\frac{3}{(x-1)^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x-3{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|\\,{|}_{2}^{4}+3\\cdot \\frac{1}{x-1}\\,{|}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)+3(\\frac{1}{3}-1) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{1})+3(-\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319139 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25ee2626-1199-5d03-8d29-49c8714aadba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25ee2626-1199-5d03-8d29-49c8714aadba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317684} :answer-field/id #uuid "41efcb76-531e-5d4e-bbdc-2d53d1b9757d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318562} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a319b5d2-0ac1-592d-b731-73b0e1569f89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318124}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a319b5d2-0ac1-592d-b731-73b0e1569f89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318124} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ab4aa44-1646-5ef0-a44c-3eb1d7c25c9b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318656}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9e232935-891f-5034-b933-fe05b7b0f382" :question/math-academy-id "q-346793" :question/problem "Express $\\frac{4x+23}{(5-2x)(x+3)}$ as a sum of partial fractions.\n\n| $\\frac{4x+23}{(5-2x)(x+3)}=\\,$ | {{field-1}} | $+$ | {{field-2}} |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $5-2x$ |  | $x+3$ |" :question/worked-solution "Our task is to find $A$ and $B$ such that\n\n$$\n\\frac{4x+23}{(5-2x)(x+3)}=\\frac{A}{5-2x}+\\frac{B}{x+3}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $(5-2x)(x+3)$ gives\n\n$$\n4x+23=A(x+3)+B(5-2x).\n$$\n\nFor $x=-3$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(-3)+23 & =A(-3+3)+B(5-2(-3)) \\\\ 11 & =11B \\\\ B & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=\\frac{5}{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(\\frac{5}{2})+23 & =A(\\frac{5}{2}+3)+B(5-2(\\frac{5}{2})) \\\\ 33 & =A(\\frac{11}{2}) \\\\ A & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{4x+23}{(5-2x)(x+3)}=\\frac{6}{5-2x}+\\frac{1}{x+3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319140 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3292633b-30ac-5c02-bb00-561e642ae309" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317723}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3292633b-30ac-5c02-bb00-561e642ae309" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317723} :answer-field/id #uuid "2861d4ee-99bc-55fd-980b-a15c95a09bea" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318506} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97177ef5-a170-5955-bfe0-424ef6f22683" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318081}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97177ef5-a170-5955-bfe0-424ef6f22683" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318081} :answer-field/id #uuid "41d65071-bc35-56ea-b328-5b09965b48e3" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318561} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3fb85f9-4ad6-5774-bff4-0de9fba7fdd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318129}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3fb85f9-4ad6-5774-bff4-0de9fba7fdd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318129} :answer-field/id #uuid "c233b400-2ce4-5120-a868-2448b5aac6f8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318842}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f2b918d3-6ba6-56f0-80d2-8d7b4daa7976" :question/math-academy-id "q-346796" :question/problem "Express $\\frac{5{x}^{2}+10x-21}{(x-1)(x+2)(2x-3)}$ as a sum of partial fractions.\n\n| $\\frac{5{x}^{2}+10x-21}{(x-1)(x+2)(2x-3)}=\\,$ | {{field-1}} | $-$ | {{field-2}} | $+$ | {{field-3}} |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $x-1$ |  | $x+2$ |  | $2x-3$ |" :question/worked-solution "Our task is to find $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\frac{5{x}^{2}+10x-21}{(x-1)(x+2)(2x-3)}=\\frac{A}{x-1}+\\frac{B}{x+2}+\\frac{C}{2x-3}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $(x-1)(x+2)(2x-3)$ gives\n\n$$\n5{x}^{2}+10x-21=A(x+2)(2x-3)+B(x-1)(2x-3)+C(x-1)(x+2).\n$$\n\nFor $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(1)^{2}+10(1)-21 & =A(1+2)(2(1)-3)+B(1-1)(2(1)-3)+C(1-1)(1+2) \\\\ -6 & =-3A \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=-2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(-2)^{2}+10(-2)-21 & =A(-2+2)(2(-2)-3)+B(-2-1)(2(-2)-3)+C(-2-1)(-2+2) \\\\ -21 & =21B \\\\ B & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=\\frac{3}{2}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+10(\\frac{3}{2})-21 & =A(\\frac{3}{2}+2)(2(\\frac{3}{2})-3)+B(\\frac{3}{2}-1)(2(\\frac{3}{2})-3)+C(\\frac{3}{2}-1)(\\frac{3}{2}+2) \\\\ \\frac{21}{4} & =\\frac{7}{4}C \\\\ C & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{5{x}^{2}+10x-21}{(x-1)(x+2)(2x-3)}=\\frac{2}{x-1}-\\frac{1}{x+2}+\\frac{3}{2x-3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319145 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b433582c-2f7d-5011-a109-56570d612329" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318174}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b433582c-2f7d-5011-a109-56570d612329" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318174} :answer-field/id #uuid "0212c1b9-9229-5284-86c3-5ed77f20f012" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318427} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09a8f9e1-4e87-56f5-963a-2d17aa1d8367" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317602}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09a8f9e1-4e87-56f5-963a-2d17aa1d8367" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317602} :answer-field/id #uuid "02faa55b-56ce-5fd7-a61f-3942784b6bf8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318430} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7917ed5-07be-504e-9ca9-f5cb015df1b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318385}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7917ed5-07be-504e-9ca9-f5cb015df1b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318385} :answer-field/id #uuid "20e3fef3-301d-5e4c-9f9c-1f104afbbca1" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318493}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cb591c3f-e5ec-5e76-968d-22d893bd432e" :question/math-academy-id "q-346871" :question/problem "What is the value of $f(t)=\\sqrt[3]{{t}^{2}+4}\\,i+{3}^{t}\\,j+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{2})\\,k$ at $t=2?$\n\n$f(2)=$\n{{field-1}} $i\\,+\\,$\n\n{{field-2}} $j\\,+\\,$\n\n{{field-3}} $k$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate, we substitute $t=2$ into the function, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & =\\sqrt[3]{{2}^{2}+4}\\,i+{3}^{2}\\,j+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{2})\\,k \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{8}\\,i+{3}^{2}\\,j+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\pi )\\,k \\\\  & =2\\,i+9\\,j+0\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319149 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4433636-de54-5170-a857-396d891596aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318131}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4433636-de54-5170-a857-396d891596aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318131} :answer-field/id #uuid "31383c4f-cfa9-5007-b57d-2e5cd4edf56b" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318525} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54c20fd1-e5d0-5ba9-99bf-7df4f0871ab5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317848}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54c20fd1-e5d0-5ba9-99bf-7df4f0871ab5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317848} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a98a53e-0ccc-5bb0-9b9b-c7afded015e5" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318682} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ac6e641-ebff-5ba3-96ab-3a7269a51765" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317654}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ac6e641-ebff-5ba3-96ab-3a7269a51765" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317654} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b92fd65-9ef1-54b2-ab0f-14aec51ef8f4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318686}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "807c4b87-85e6-5eed-9e1d-1e100a9b4aab" :question/math-academy-id "q-346957" :question/problem "Express $\\frac{5}{({x}^{2}+4)(x+2)}$ as a sum of partial fractions.\n\n| $\\frac{5}{({x}^{2}+4)(x+2)}=\\,$ | {{field-1}}$\\,x\\,+$\n{{field-2}} | $+$ | {{field-3}} |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $8({x}^{2}+4)$ |  | $8(x+2)$ |" :question/worked-solution "Our task is to find $A$, $B$, and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{({x}^{2}+4)(x+2)}=\\frac{Ax+B}{{x}^{2}+4}+\\frac{C}{x+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $({x}^{2}+4)(x+2)$ gives\n\n$$\n5=(Ax+B)(x+2)+C({x}^{2}+4).\n$$\n\nFor $x=-2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =C(4+4) \\\\ 5 & =8C \\\\ C & =\\frac{5}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=0$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =(A(0)+B)(0+2)+\\frac{5}{8}((0)^{2}+4) \\\\ 5 & =2B+\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 2B & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ B & =\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =(A(1)+\\frac{5}{4})(1+2)+\\frac{5}{8}((1)^{2}+4) \\\\ 5 & =3A+\\frac{15}{4}+\\frac{25}{8} \\\\ 3A & =-\\frac{15}{8} \\\\ A & =-\\frac{5}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{({x}^{2}+4)(x+2)} & =\\frac{(-\\frac{5}{8}x+\\frac{5}{4})}{{x}^{2}+4}+\\frac{(\\frac{5}{8})}{x+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-5\\,x+10}{8({x}^{2}+4)}+\\frac{5}{8(x+2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319152 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e62c0ae-d8d1-54c4-b59e-37ff1225c3c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}(\\frac{40}{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317998}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e62c0ae-d8d1-54c4-b59e-37ff1225c3c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}(\\frac{40}{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317998} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2b15ad3-db26-57a4-ae32-bd15d4341f5f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318783}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1fc7feda-9975-5f67-aaa7-81f6387a8c6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-347077" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{3}^{6}\\frac{2x+2}{(x-2)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+2}{(x-2)(x+4)} & =\\frac{A}{(x-2)}+\\frac{B}{(x+4)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n2x+2=A(x+4)+B(x-2).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-4,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-4)+2 & =A(-4+4)+B(-4-2) \\\\ -6 & =-6B \\\\ B & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=2,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(2)+2 & =A(2+4)+B(2-2) \\\\ 6 & =6A \\\\ A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2x+2}{(x-2)(x+4)}=\\frac{1}{x-2}+\\frac{1}{x+4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+2}{(x-2)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{x-2}+\\frac{1}{x+4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+2}{(x-2)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x-2)(x+4)|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{6}\\frac{2x+2}{(x-2)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x-2)(x+4)|{|}_{3}^{6} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(40)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7) \\\\  & =ln\\,(\\frac{40}{7}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319153 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01f33917-c3bd-5b12-9b6b-0ce6f8b325a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}(t)+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317576}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01f33917-c3bd-5b12-9b6b-0ce6f8b325a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}(t)+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317576} :answer-field/id #uuid "a200f1e4-6bd5-5695-b6f2-1b9d16078323" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318782} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02080071-87ef-54d7-ace2-86ed19a7a892" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}(3t)-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317578}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02080071-87ef-54d7-ace2-86ed19a7a892" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}(3t)-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317578} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd5791a6-a1f2-5cc2-8148-a1c683f40b10" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318837}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "da0cb5eb-230c-5c67-a533-8ac850a1bfba" :question/math-academy-id "q-347095" :question/problem "Find $F(t)$ given that \n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=⟨-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(t)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(t)\\,,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)⟩\n$$\n\n and $F(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨4,\\,-2⟩.$\n\n$F(t)=⟨${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$⟩$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that \n\n$$\nF(t)=\\int {F}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int ⟨-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(t)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(t)\\,,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)⟩\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(t)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(t)\\,,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)⟩\\text{d}t & =⟨\\int -\\operatorname{csc}⁡(t)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{csc}⁡(t)+{c}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe initial condition $F(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨4,\\,-2⟩$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+{c}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})+{c}_{2}⟩ & =⟨4,\\,-2⟩ \\\\ ⟨1+{c}_{1},\\,-1+{c}_{2}⟩ & =⟨4,\\,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${c}_{1}=3$ and ${c}_{2}=-1.$ Consequently\n\n$$\nF(t)=⟨\\operatorname{csc}⁡(t)+3,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-1⟩.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319155 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b79c6239-a2b4-5adb-bff4-04a647c99f4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318181}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b79c6239-a2b4-5adb-bff4-04a647c99f4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318181} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1ff960c-dde6-51c4-9221-8694e66bf0d3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318897}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1effe8ba-c512-5938-b5b0-96467f99280a" :question/math-academy-id "q-347129" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}/9}^{\\infty }\\frac{15}{9{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+(kx)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(kx)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}/9}^{\\infty }\\frac{15}{9{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =15\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}/9}^{a}\\frac{1}{9{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =15\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}/9}^{a}\\frac{1}{(3x)^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =15\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x)]}_{\\sqrt{3}/9}^{a} \\\\  & =5\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3a)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})) \\\\  & =5\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3a)-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3a)-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/9f/9f882cb5102270dfe4bef256782c18aaeeca3518f564cd188298040237e30f62.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}/9}^{\\infty }\\frac{15}{9{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3a)-\\frac{5\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{5\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319164 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a7d255f-76ed-5d4d-9c15-25da71cd5728" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317871}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a7d255f-76ed-5d4d-9c15-25da71cd5728" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317871} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff050c97-a6d3-556d-b091-7af92711e18e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318957}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8b7d4a2c-14e4-5bf2-b20c-66932dfe09d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-347189" :question/problem "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1),\\,\\frac{3}{t}⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the speed $|v|$ of the particle at time $t=3.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe speed at time $t=3$ is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{t+1},\\,-\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=3,$ we substitute in the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(3) & =⟨\\frac{1}{3+1},\\,-\\frac{3}{{3}^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{4},\\,-\\frac{1}{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed at $t=3$ we calculate the magnitude of $v(3),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|v(3)| & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{16}+\\frac{1}{9}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{25}{144}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319173 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "adbb49ea-0d78-52cf-ba8f-13798c4ec7b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318154}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "adbb49ea-0d78-52cf-ba8f-13798c4ec7b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318154} :answer-field/id #uuid "1abfac5e-bbd1-5a4f-a845-423b3e81013e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318481} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b514cd4-62f9-597b-89cf-e6b5db7905e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317657}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b514cd4-62f9-597b-89cf-e6b5db7905e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317657} :answer-field/id #uuid "3cc67bbe-7474-5dc8-b7db-9eaf9678451a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318548}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "226f50bc-b843-5766-9cf3-7f8c6585337d" :question/math-academy-id "q-347229" :question/problem "Find the range of values of $k$ for which the series\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{9}^{kn}}{{3}^{8n}}$\nconverges. Do not round the answer.\n\n$k$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the series so that it's in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }a{r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{9}^{kn}}{{3}^{8n}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{{9}^{k}}{{3}^{8}})}^{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{{9}^{k}}{{9}^{4}})}^{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{({9}^{k-4})}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that this is a geometric series with a common ratio of $r={9}^{k-4}.$ \n\nFor the series to converge, we require $|r|<1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{9}^{k-4}| & <1 \\\\ {9}^{k-4} & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we could remove the absolute value bars because ${9}^{k-4}$ is always positive.\n\nThe equation ${9}^{k-4}=1$ has the solution $k=4.$ Now, since ${9}^{k-4}$ increases as $k$ increases, the solution to ${9}^{k-4}<1$ must be $k<4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319182 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "797bc2a6-2341-55dd-873d-4b6ac0c84ad2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317977}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "797bc2a6-2341-55dd-873d-4b6ac0c84ad2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317977} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f8ca8af-f6ee-533e-b29c-ab3346bd7b7f" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318518} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2ab4558-3a85-5515-bef0-c07596fb4c72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318169}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2ab4558-3a85-5515-bef0-c07596fb4c72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318169} :answer-field/id #uuid "974cc391-37e1-57a4-94d9-469fa48eb9be" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318759} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3046fc10-9d94-5a65-9f58-734286a4697c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317711}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3046fc10-9d94-5a65-9f58-734286a4697c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317711} :answer-field/id #uuid "c351f8a2-9df9-5565-bda3-25f3df12e50e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318845}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a788bb34-e61a-56de-b053-7c383ccbe7d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-347485" :question/problem "Find the second-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{2}(x)$ of $f(x)=\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)$ about $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\n${P}_{2}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,+$\n{{field-3}}${(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "To find the second-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we plug $a=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}){(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to work out $f(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),$ ${f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),$ and ${f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x) & ⟹ &  & f(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡(x)} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =3 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{cos}^{3}⁡(x)} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now plug the results for $f(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),$ ${f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),$ and ${f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ into our second-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =\\frac{3}{2}+3(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}+3(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+3{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319184 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ab5ee3f-1c0c-55aa-a987-3e47b79d749c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317934}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ab5ee3f-1c0c-55aa-a987-3e47b79d749c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317934} :answer-field/id #uuid "866942c4-c6fb-53e8-9a41-30c0a30ba46a" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318716} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f8aa453-c5a3-5258-a02c-823abe0262ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317770}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f8aa453-c5a3-5258-a02c-823abe0262ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317770} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7fc80ba-c574-50e8-872c-fb745605e2d9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318912} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e60bc73c-1331-5696-a1c2-d3297d81e49e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318334}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e60bc73c-1331-5696-a1c2-d3297d81e49e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318334} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8a7c333-1ff0-5461-9df5-9b27ba61db41" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318943}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6392f233-064f-5acb-81b9-e3a0f8e2d3bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-347514" :question/problem "Find the interval of convergence for the following series:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{4}^{n}(5n-2)}=\\frac{x}{4\\cdot 3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}\\cdot 8}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{4}^{3}\\cdot 13}+⋯\n$$\n\nThe interval of convergence is\n{{field-1}}\n$\\le x$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{4}^{n}(5n-2)}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{x}^{n+1}}{{4}^{n+1}(5n+3)}\\cdot \\frac{{4}^{n}(5n-2)}{{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{x(5n-2)}{4(5n+3)}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{4}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{5n-2}{5n+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{4}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{|x|}{4}<1\\,⟹\\,|x|<4.\n$$\n\nSo, the radius of convergence is $4,$ and the series converges for any $x$ that lies in the open interval $(-4,4).$\n\nThe ratio test gives no conclusion about the convergence of the series when $L=1.$ So, when $|x|=4,$ we do not know if the series converges. This means that the endpoints $x=-4$ and $x=4$ need to be investigated separately using another test for convergence.\n\n- When $x=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{4}^{n}(5n-2)} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{4}^{n}}{{4}^{n}(5n-2)} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{5n-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{1}{13}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo see if this series converges, we use the limit comparison test, with ${a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{5n-2}$ and ${b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{n}.$ We observe that ${a}_{n}$ and ${b}_{n}$ are positive, and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{b}_{n}}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{1}{5n-2})}{(\\frac{1}{n})}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{n}{5n-2}=\\frac{1}{5},\n$$\n\n \nwhich is positive and finite. Hence, the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ and ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ either *both* converge or *both* diverge.\nSince ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n}$ is divergent, the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{5n-2}$ is also divergent by the limit comparison test. So, the endpoint $x=4$ is not included in our interval.\n\n- When $x=-4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{4}^{n}(5n-2)} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-4)^{n}}{{4}^{n}(5n-2)} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{5n-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{8}-\\frac{1}{13}+⋯,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the alternating series test. So, the endpoint $x=-4$ is included in our interval.\n\nTherefore, the interval of convergence is $[-4,4),$ or equivalently, $-4\\le x<4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319185 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b2a878a-69e0-5281-8468-b64406ed262d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317814}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b2a878a-69e0-5281-8468-b64406ed262d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317814} :answer-field/id #uuid "2019e334-f3f9-55a3-b58a-3ed19a8b58f3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318490}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6ed4d6fb-9e42-5a64-ad35-6dec89e5b2ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-347518" :question/problem "Find the radius of convergence $R$ for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{n}^{3}(x-2)^{n}=(x-2)+{2}^{3}(x-2)^{2}+{3}^{3}(x-2)^{3}+⋯\\,.\n$$\n\n$R=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}={n}^{3}(x-2)^{n}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(n+1)^{3}(x-2)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}(x-2)^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(n+1)^{3}(x-2)}{{n}^{3}}| \\\\  & =|x-2|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{n+1}{n})}^{3} \\\\  & =|x-2|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =|x-2|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied if $|x-2|<1.$ Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319186 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1670a3cf-9743-54e9-8189-836f7fa55225" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-15 \\\\ 23 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317635} {:answer/id #uuid "3666f00b-c376-5f08-a75e-4782964f8dbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -\\frac{37}{3} \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317740} {:answer/id #uuid "5a7e0096-3b52-5db5-b3ac-0b6ebf5f8c04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ 26 \\\\ 27\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317872} {:answer/id #uuid "83730cd0-d5e3-5d0a-a49e-ed307de58909" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 14 \\\\ 36\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318007} {:answer/id #uuid "f864fa30-dd03-571e-9a9a-36c8567c5ef6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -34 \\\\ -36\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318388}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f864fa30-dd03-571e-9a9a-36c8567c5ef6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -34 \\\\ -36\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318388} :answer-field/id #uuid "56f47f49-1ef5-57cb-904a-c3e3077963fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318611}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "26071956-0b9c-584b-9262-4d72b8945fc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-34760" :question/problem "Find the vector $x$, if $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}},$ and \n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -\\frac{8}{3} \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix},\\,{b}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix},\\,[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We know that any vector in  $\\text{Span}{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ has the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -\\frac{8}{3} \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the coordinates relative to the basis $\\,B.$ Since we are told that $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\underbrace{(\\frac{3}{2})}_{{x}_{1}}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -\\frac{8}{3} \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}+\\underbrace{(-3)}_{{x}_{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-18 \\\\ -30 \\\\ -54\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -34 \\\\ -36\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319188 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3321115a-1180-5c5b-a989-d9b89f611d25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317727}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3321115a-1180-5c5b-a989-d9b89f611d25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317727} :answer-field/id #uuid "85aee846-359f-5b08-a9de-8f4adcb72510" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318714} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dec84e90-2e10-5b17-9206-5ad94172a7d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318309}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dec84e90-2e10-5b17-9206-5ad94172a7d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318309} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4350e71-8c5c-57c1-9d01-7580966bb9fe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318813} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d0fe7eca-c66a-5883-af97-4d540b7c6068" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318256}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0fe7eca-c66a-5883-af97-4d540b7c6068" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318256} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4de9581-0ac4-5575-8ce8-8bdb9a196a90" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318877}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "756a953d-5a94-5865-8d60-c69c435be895" :question/math-academy-id "q-347614" :question/problem "Find the first three non-zero terms in the Taylor expansion of $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ about the point $x=2.$\n\n$\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}=${{field-1}}\n$-\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$(x-2)\\,+\\,$\n{{field-3}}\n$(x-2)^{2}-⋯$" :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ and $a=2.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}={x}^{-2} \\\\ f(2) & ={2}^{-2}=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}=-2{x}^{-3} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =-2(2)^{-3}=-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}}=6{x}^{-4} \\\\ {f}^{″}(2) & =6(2)^{-4}=\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{4}+(-\\frac{1}{4})(x-2)+\\frac{(\\frac{3}{8})}{2!}(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{(-\\frac{3}{4})}{3!}(x-2)^{3}+⋯ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}(x-2)+\\frac{3}{16}(x-2)^{2}-⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319189 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30cd0d9b-5fe9-51b3-9519-02b039db9514" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317715}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30cd0d9b-5fe9-51b3-9519-02b039db9514" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317715} :answer-field/id #uuid "89037d38-162e-5bd4-86ef-575cf06fd386" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318723}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "347dab61-bb37-50f6-ab92-42466a2565f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-347615" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25} & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{25}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{(x/5)^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{25}\\cdot 5{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{5})|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{5})) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}(0-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{3\\pi }{10}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{10}+\\frac{3\\pi }{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319192 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a003a1d1-8ee3-58b9-861c-8527f9522a22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318112}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a003a1d1-8ee3-58b9-861c-8527f9522a22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318112} :answer-field/id #uuid "2837f48b-1a7c-56c4-a073-cfae4a30010a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318505}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5fcea307-0381-5580-bd2c-78e4cb1e7349" :question/math-academy-id "q-347621" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let  $u=5+{x}^{8}$,  then $\\text{d}u=8{x}^{7}\\text{d}x.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{u}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5+{x}^{8}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now evaluate the first of the improper integrals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{(-\\frac{1}{5+{x}^{8}})|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{5+0}-\\frac{1}{5+{a}^{8}}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{5+{a}^{8}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}+0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **odd** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319194 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "897332b3-8c34-50c6-9aee-61a82f3ed1e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{219}{200}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318028}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "897332b3-8c34-50c6-9aee-61a82f3ed1e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{219}{200}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318028} :answer-field/id #uuid "be66636a-8532-51b6-aa7b-719f82340b31" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318839}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5d0330bc-2c1d-5dd3-9a79-e9c8a3180d87" :question/math-academy-id "q-347635" :question/problem "Use the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of $\\sqrt{x}$ about $x=1$ to approximate the value of $\\sqrt{1.2}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\sqrt{1.2}≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ and $a=1.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\sqrt{x}={x}^{1/2} \\\\ f(1) & ={1}^{1/2}=1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{2}){x}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x} & =1+\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)+\\frac{(-1/4)}{2!}(x-1)^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)-\\frac{1}{8}(x-1)^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=1.2$ into the above to approximate $\\sqrt{1.2}.$ Since\n\n$$\n1.2-1=\\frac{1}{5},\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1.2} & ≈1+\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{5})-\\frac{1}{8}{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{10}-\\frac{1}{200} \\\\  & =\\frac{219}{200}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319195 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07472bd2-a9cd-522a-9f52-0b681eccc11e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{51}{50}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317595}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07472bd2-a9cd-522a-9f52-0b681eccc11e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{51}{50}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317595} :answer-field/id #uuid "3958f52b-2459-596b-beba-67dac94e6558" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318542}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2c3ff0dd-0084-5332-a5b5-9ae0db80dd2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-347637" :question/problem "Use the first two non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ about $x=\\pi $ to approximate the value of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\pi +0.2).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\pi +0.2)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ and $a=\\pi .$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x \\\\ f(\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\pi )=-1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\pi )\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\pi )=0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x{tan}^{2}⁡x+{sec}^{3}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}(\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\pi ){tan}^{2}⁡(\\pi )+{sec}^{3}⁡(\\pi )=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =-1+(0)(x-\\pi )+\\frac{(-1)}{2!}(x-\\pi )^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =-1-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\pi )^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=\\pi +0.2$ into the above to approximate $\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\pi +0.2).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\pi +0.2) & ≈-1-\\frac{1}{2}{(\\pi +0.2-\\pi )}^{2} \\\\  & =-1-\\frac{1}{2}(0.2)^{2} \\\\  & =-1-\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{25}) \\\\  & =-1-\\frac{1}{50} \\\\  & =-\\frac{51}{50}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319201 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19cfa2e0-d54c-572f-b8bd-4f33e0950b64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317650}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19cfa2e0-d54c-572f-b8bd-4f33e0950b64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317650} :answer-field/id #uuid "31ca60d2-e433-59b2-8aa9-7176db6fedfe" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318526} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cee93a28-42f7-5a58-94e0-48f329383dc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318249}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cee93a28-42f7-5a58-94e0-48f329383dc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318249} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb3aa437-3a1f-5460-9133-7d610bb54bed" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318832}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a87c6bb4-7854-5fc3-a9aa-5ab23d8f133c" :question/math-academy-id "q-347770" :question/problem "Consider the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{5}^{n+1}}{{x}^{n}},$ where $x$ is a positive real number and $n\\ge 1.$ Find the values of $x$ for which the sequence converges.\n\n$x$\n\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nFirst, we rewrite the sequence in the form ${a}_{n}=a\\cdot {r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{{5}^{n+1}}{{x}^{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot {5}^{n}}{{x}^{n}} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{{5}^{n}}{{x}^{n}} \\\\  & =5{(\\frac{5}{x})}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe see that this is a geometric sequence with the common ratio $r=\\frac{5}{x}.$ Since $x$ is positive,  $r$ is also positive. So, the convergence condition is $0<r\\le 1.$ \n\nTherefore, the  sequence converges for \n\n$$\n0<\\frac{5}{x}\\le 1\\,⟹\\,x\\ge 5.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319202 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57db562f-825a-5c06-9c18-a4185051c072" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317861}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57db562f-825a-5c06-9c18-a4185051c072" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317861} :answer-field/id #uuid "5672edce-69fb-5725-aa5f-85f880ba3571" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318610}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "95cfa877-83ba-5877-86e4-7c000e0d1f2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-347839" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3y\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x),\\,y\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=1.\n$$\n\nYou are given that $y=A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$\n\n$A=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We are given that $y=A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=1.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=1$ into the solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) \\\\ 1 & =Asin\\,(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\ 1 & =Asin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ 1 & =A(1) \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319211 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc63ae4e-cfaa-53ce-820b-6b955f4e241e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\pi /8}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318407}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc63ae4e-cfaa-53ce-820b-6b955f4e241e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\pi /8}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318407} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c3bf0c6-3804-5ad7-986b-8aafdaaff36d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318485}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dc5a81b5-b9fd-57fc-867d-023dfe7eb508" :question/math-academy-id "q-347926" :question/problem "![](images/2d/2da83e2ec620d3920c4378a77785a9ef2802dd1d06badcf4852be2263f68264e.png)\n\nFind the arc length $L$ of the polar curve $r=6{e}^{\\theta }$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{8}$ (shown in blue in the diagram above). Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=6\\sqrt{2}($\n{{field-1}} $)$" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=6{e}^{\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=6{e}^{\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sqrt{{(6{e}^{\\theta })}^{2}+{(6{e}^{\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sqrt{36{e}^{2\\theta }+36{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sqrt{72{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{e}^{\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}\\,{e}^{\\theta }{|}_{0}^{\\pi /8} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}({e}^{\\pi /8}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}({e}^{\\pi /8}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319224 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "413af386-018a-5a79-9059-adefc2791bd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317776}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "413af386-018a-5a79-9059-adefc2791bd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317776} :answer-field/id #uuid "487a3ee4-df99-519f-a750-20dbe1bce54e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318584}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f8e9f1bb-dfe9-519e-8bb3-e8a484b04ce5" :question/math-academy-id "q-348086" :question/problem "![](images/12/121012fc5671ada263e83ec6d3a897d187ecb3a9d56c8e4331a3c0c608a92622.png)\n\nFind the arc length $L$ of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{t}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡t+{t}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{t}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319225 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63e7b79b-6298-5b7e-aa80-09f258e9f2ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317907}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63e7b79b-6298-5b7e-aa80-09f258e9f2ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317907} :answer-field/id #uuid "1499962a-f058-50a8-a54d-83b434d7fef1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318468}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0c09908c-2ee7-5200-b887-daef1f475ca5" :question/math-academy-id "q-348088" :question/problem "![](images/be/be9e314ed11882842bbf9a74deeb3a994596c8275a4e854ffc66327fa0008058.png)\n\nFind the arc length $L$ of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ].\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{{(t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{{t}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡t+{t}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{{t}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{(2\\pi )^{2}}{2}-0 \\\\  & =2{\\pi }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319226 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96314937-c536-5bae-9e39-cf844b51de53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318078}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96314937-c536-5bae-9e39-cf844b51de53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318078} :answer-field/id #uuid "52e1c87e-231d-557c-9bd5-7ae92841d688" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318603}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0877a5c5-e4d1-511a-acaa-d1f39d42fd10" :question/math-academy-id "q-348093" :question/problem "For the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ),$ evaluate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n${\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta \\,=\\,\\pi /4}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the rule for differentiating parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the numerator and denominator at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot 0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+(-1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot 0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-(-1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,{|}_{\\theta \\,=\\,\\pi /4} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319231 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "639260c0-53c6-59b3-b1e6-e1986d9bc6a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317904}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "639260c0-53c6-59b3-b1e6-e1986d9bc6a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317904} :answer-field/id #uuid "5974d30a-ec36-5215-95ba-60fa8dcde0ca" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318618} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85532a8a-4f40-5913-9cd2-e0f803e535aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318019}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85532a8a-4f40-5913-9cd2-e0f803e535aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318019} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a671c7d-1105-5123-bb57-8d5976a419d3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318680} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e39ced00-b89f-5c02-9cc9-48217c75aed7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318321}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e39ced00-b89f-5c02-9cc9-48217c75aed7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318321} :answer-field/id #uuid "921ca22d-21df-5fb0-b1b9-8be6064c63ba" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318744}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ad040194-971d-54b7-8f33-bceb77563250" :question/math-academy-id "q-348121" :question/problem "Find the third-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{3}(x)$ of  ${sin}^{2}⁡x$ about $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${P}_{3}(x)=\\frac{3}{4}\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,-$\n{{field-2}}${(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})}^{2}\\,-$\n\n{{field-3}}${(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "To find the third-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ we plug $a=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{3}(x) & =f(\\frac{\\pi }{3})+{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\frac{1}{2!}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})}^{2}+{f}^{‴}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\frac{1}{3!}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})}^{3} \\\\  & =f(\\frac{\\pi }{3})+{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}){(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}{f}^{‴}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}){(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to compute $f(\\frac{\\pi }{3}),$ ${f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}),$ ${f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}),$ and ${f}^{‴}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={sin}^{2}⁡x & ⟹ &  & f(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & ={sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})={(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}^{2}=\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x & ⟹ &  & {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x & ⟹ &  & {f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=2(-\\frac{1}{2})=-1 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x & ⟹ &  & {f}^{‴}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=-4(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=-2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we plug the results for $f(\\frac{\\pi }{3}),$ ${f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}),$ ${f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}),$ and ${f}^{‴}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ into our third-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{3}(x) & =\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+\\frac{(-1)}{2}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})}^{2}+\\frac{(-2\\sqrt{3})}{6}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\frac{1}{2}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})}^{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319232 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c452c45-e9e5-5b9c-a9dc-12ef821fd6bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}{sec}^{3}(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317610}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c452c45-e9e5-5b9c-a9dc-12ef821fd6bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}{sec}^{3}(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317610} :answer-field/id #uuid "051bb36f-1d11-5b10-b2ec-04d162731ca0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318437}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fcdb0d99-88c2-5541-9046-61f9f5e56a06" :question/math-academy-id "q-348139" :question/problem "For the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ calculate $\\frac{{d}^{2}y}{d{x}^{2}}$ as a function of $\\theta .$\n\n$\\frac{{d}^{2}y}{d{x}^{2}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{d}^{2}y}{d{x}^{2}}=\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{d\\theta })}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ and $\\frac{dx}{d\\theta }.$ \n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nas parametric equations of a curve in terms of $\\theta .$ \n\nUsing the fact that $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,y=3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nThen, to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ we use the rule\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta ).\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting into our formula for the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta ))}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{sec}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{sec}^{3}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319239 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea0640d9-c3e5-5ddf-9df9-feeb259286d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318345}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea0640d9-c3e5-5ddf-9df9-feeb259286d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318345} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b191dcb-cda9-53a5-951a-047fd95e08bd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318657}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "03e05686-97cf-5c15-b403-a4caf1953954" :question/math-academy-id "q-348275" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-1}^{1/3}\\frac{7}{4}x\\sqrt[3]{6x+6}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=6x+6.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{6}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe also note that $x=\\frac{1}{6}(u-6).$ \n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get\n\n| **$x$** | $-1$ | $1/3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **$u$** | $0$ | $8$ |\n\nWe can now solve the integral using our new variable $u,$ remembering to use the new limits of integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1/3}\\frac{7}{4}x\\sqrt[3]{6x+6}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{8}\\frac{7}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}(u-6)\\cdot {u}^{1/3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{144}{\\int }_{0}^{8}({u}^{4/3}-6{u}^{1/3})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{144}{(\\frac{1}{(7/3)}{u}^{7/3}-6\\cdot \\frac{1}{(4/3)}{u}^{4/3})|}_{0}^{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{144}{(\\frac{3}{7}{u}^{7/3}-\\frac{9}{2}{u}^{4/3})|}_{0}^{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{144}[(\\frac{3}{7}\\cdot {8}^{7/3}-\\frac{9}{2}\\cdot {8}^{4/3})-(\\frac{3}{7}\\cdot {0}^{7/3}-\\frac{9}{2}\\cdot {0}^{4/3})] \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{144}(\\frac{384}{7}-72) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{144}\\cdot (-\\frac{120}{7}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319242 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "730ade57-923a-5b77-b9d1-e09ab5c9256d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317960}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "730ade57-923a-5b77-b9d1-e09ab5c9256d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317960} :answer-field/id #uuid "acfb67b2-49ba-5e80-bd44-691f651b8f48" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318797}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4037d146-183a-5606-a4b5-d0636799ff9e" :question/math-academy-id "q-348282" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-3}^{-2}\\frac{48(x+2)}{{({x}^{2}+4x)}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $u={x}^{2}+4x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x+4\\,⟹\\,2(x+2)\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get\n\n| **$x$** | $-3$ | $-2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **$u$** | $-3$ | $-4$ |\n\nWe can now solve the integral using our new variable $u,$ remembering to use the new limits of integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{-2}\\frac{48(x+2)}{{({x}^{2}+4x)}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =24{\\int }_{-3}^{-2}\\frac{1}{{({x}^{2}+4x)}^{3}}\\cdot 2(x+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =24{\\int }_{-3}^{-4}\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =24{\\int }_{-3}^{-4}{u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =24\\cdot {(-\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-2})|}_{-3}^{-4} \\\\  & ={(-\\frac{12}{{u}^{2}})|}_{-3}^{-4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{12}{(-4)^{2}}-(-\\frac{12}{(-3)^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{12}{16}-(-\\frac{12}{9}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319243 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f92673c-93e1-5be6-93ae-6ffaadf6d7db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318055}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f92673c-93e1-5be6-93ae-6ffaadf6d7db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318055} :answer-field/id #uuid "d48ddeb2-9295-55b0-9f0d-6c6f6b11577f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318875}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "eae00a34-daa5-5a67-90d9-6bc0c0ecdb5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-348283" :question/problem "A particle $Q$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t\\ge 0$ is given by $v(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ At time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ the particle is located at the position $x=1.$ \nFind the position of the particle at time $t=\\pi .$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe position of the particle at $t=\\pi $ is $x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The position of the particle at $t=\\pi $ is equal to its position at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\pi ].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(\\pi )=x(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nCarrying out the computations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(\\pi ) & =1+{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =1+{[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})]}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =1+([-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4})]-[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{4})]) \\\\  & =1+(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =1+(-(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})+1) \\\\  & =1+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+1 \\\\  & =2+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the position of the particle at $t=\\pi $ is $x=2+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319245 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73575143-4460-538c-8aa1-056786aecc43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317962}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73575143-4460-538c-8aa1-056786aecc43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317962} :answer-field/id #uuid "8176f5f0-47d6-5f0f-ba47-63f839d87ff0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318706}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2a0c50af-6c83-593f-8570-9f596da85472" :question/math-academy-id "q-348295" :question/problem "A curve has parametric equations $x={t}^{2}+1,\\,y={t}^{3}+4.$ Given that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3}{2}t,$ calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ at the point where $t=2.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,2}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To compute $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}},$ we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{3}{2}t,$ and we can calculate $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}({t}^{2}+1)=2t\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{3}{2}t)}{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{2})}{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate at $t=2,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,2}=\\frac{3}{4(2)}=\\frac{3}{8}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319246 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94afa6f7-9ec3-5674-aaf4-34a2e1a3c792" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{192\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318073}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94afa6f7-9ec3-5674-aaf4-34a2e1a3c792" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{192\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318073} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e9a7e53-8eec-5495-85b5-fb5492802a0f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318692}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "88eff08e-1bbb-529f-965a-96596e4595fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-348298" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=6t-2{t}^{3},\\,y=6{t}^{2},\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nDetermine the surface area $S$ generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$S=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6t-2{t}^{3})=6-6{t}^{2}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{t}^{2})=12t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}6{t}^{2}\\sqrt{{(6-6{t}^{2})}^{2}+{(12t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}6{t}^{2}\\sqrt{36-72{t}^{2}+36{t}^{4}+144{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}6{t}^{2}\\sqrt{36{t}^{4}+72{t}^{2}+36}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, factoring the expression $36{t}^{4}+72{t}^{2}+36,$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}6{t}^{2}\\sqrt{36({t}^{4}+2{t}^{2}+1)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}6{t}^{2}\\cdot 6\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+2{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =72\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{({t}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =72\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\cdot |{t}^{2}+1|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =72\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}({t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =72\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{4}+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =72\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{4}+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =72\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}+\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =72\\pi [(\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{3})-(\\frac{0}{5}+\\frac{0}{3})] \\\\  & =72\\pi \\cdot \\frac{8}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{192\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319248 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70131d8e-e110-5e84-bcff-45651751b52d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317953}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70131d8e-e110-5e84-bcff-45651751b52d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317953} :answer-field/id #uuid "805f7f24-9fb6-5e8f-8459-868c76a93452" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318701}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ab4d806a-76c8-563a-a10d-c7fabb39c7b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-348304" :question/problem "![](images/7a/7ae65c873838ede4cce89c953ff2654fa1e58e65fdc87896c8ba85bbf947be20.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nshown above, calculate the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t}{(-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,\\pi /4} & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]
